<h2 id="part-e">Part E: Returns &amp; Quality</h2>
<h3 id="s20">20. Customer Returns</h3>
<p>Authorised Retailers remain responsible for managing returns, refunds, replacements and other remedies requested by customers who purchased Apex Feeds products directly from that Retailer. Where a customer purchases an Apex Feeds product from an Authorised Retailer, the Retailer remains the customer’s point of purchase and should manage the customer’s initial return or complaint in accordance with applicable Australian Consumer Law and the Retailer’s own lawful returns procedures. Apex Feeds will reasonably support Authorised Retailers in resolving genuine product-quality, manufacturing or safety concerns relating to Apex Feeds products.</p>
<h4 id="s20-1">20.1 Purchases Made From a Retailer</h4>
<p>Customers who purchase Apex Feeds products from an Authorised Retailer should ordinarily return the product to the Retailer from which it was purchased. Apex Feeds cannot ordinarily issue a direct refund for the customer’s retail purchase where Apex Feeds was not the seller and did not receive the customer’s payment. Where appropriate, Apex Feeds may assist the Retailer in assessing whether the complaint relates to an Apex Feeds product-quality or manufacturing issue.</p>
<h4 id="s20-2">20.2 Proof of Purchase</h4>
<p>Retailers may require reasonable proof that the product was purchased from their business before providing a return, refund, replacement or other remedy. Reasonable proof may include a receipt, tax invoice, electronic transaction record, customer account history or other reliable evidence of purchase. Nothing in this clause permits a Retailer to refuse a remedy where applicable law requires otherwise merely because a particular form of receipt is unavailable.</p>
<h4 id="s20-3">20.3 Change of Mind</h4>
<p>Unless required by applicable law or voluntarily permitted by the Retailer, Apex Feeds does not require Authorised Retailers to accept change-of-mind returns for Apex Feeds food, feed, supplement or other consumable products.</p>
<p>Examples of circumstances that will not ordinarily constitute an Apex Feeds product defect include where the customer:</p>
<ul>
<li>purchased the wrong product or quantity;</li>
<li>changed their mind;</li>
<li>no longer requires the product;</li>
<li>ordered more product than required;</li>
<li>states that their animal refuses to consume the product;</li>
<li>prefers another product, flavour, formulation or feeding method; or</li>
<li>otherwise seeks to return a correctly supplied and non-defective product for reasons unrelated to its quality or conformity.</li>
</ul>
<p>Retailers remain free to offer a more generous change-of-mind policy at their own cost if they choose.</p>
<h4 id="s20-4">20.4 Retailer Discretionary Refunds</h4>
<p>A Retailer may choose to refund, replace, exchange or otherwise compensate a customer as a matter of customer service even where no legal obligation or Apex Feeds product defect has been established. Such a decision is a matter for the Retailer. A discretionary remedy provided by the Retailer does not automatically create an entitlement to reimbursement, replacement or wholesale credit from Apex Feeds. Any subsequent claim against Apex Feeds will be assessed independently under Sections 21–24.</p>
<h4 id="s20-5">20.5 Genuine Product Concerns</h4>
<p>\Where a customer reports mould, contamination, spoilage, abnormal deterioration, packaging failure, foreign material or another potential manufacturing or product-quality issue, the Retailer should obtain sufficient information to allow the matter to be reasonably assessed.</p>
<p>Where available, this should include:</p>
<ul>
<li>the product concerned;</li>
<li>proof of purchase;</li>
<li>photographs or video;</li>
<li>Batch Number (BN);</li>
<li>Best Before Date (BBD);</li>
<li>when the product was purchased;</li>
<li>when the issue was identified;</li>
<li>whether the product had been opened or prepared;</li>
<li>relevant storage or handling information; and</li>
<li>a reasonable description of the issue.</li>
</ul>
<p>The Retailer should then manage the customer’s immediate consumer-law obligations as applicable and submit any corresponding wholesale claim to Apex Feeds in accordance with Section 21.</p>
<h4 id="s20-6">20.6 Reporting Period for Customer Product Concerns</h4>
<p>Retailers should encourage customers to report an apparent product-quality issue as soon as reasonably practicable after discovering it. Apex Feeds may consider the time elapsed between purchase, opening, discovery and reporting when investigating the cause of a product concern.</p>
<p>A delay in reporting does not automatically invalidate a genuine claim where applicable law provides otherwise, but Apex Feeds may reasonably take into account whether the delay has affected the ability to establish the cause of the issue.</p>
<h4 id="s20-7">20.7 Opened Products</h4>
<p>The fact that an Apex Feeds product has been opened does not prevent a customer from raising a genuine product-quality concern. However, an opened product must not be returned to normal retail inventory or resold as new stock. Where an opened product is relevant to a quality claim, the Retailer should retain it or request that the customer retain it where reasonably practicable until sufficient evidence has been obtained or Apex Feeds advises whether physical return is required.</p>
<h4 id="s20-8">20.8 Products Requiring Physical Return</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may reasonably require the affected product or packaging to be retained or physically returned where examination is necessary to determine the cause of an alleged defect. A physical return will not be required where it would be unreasonable, unsafe or unnecessary in the circumstances or where sufficient evidence is otherwise available. Where Apex Feeds requires physical return of a product for the purpose of assessing a potentially valid Apex Feeds defect claim, the appropriate return arrangements will be determined having regard to the circumstances and applicable law.</p>
<h4 id="s20-9">20.9 Customer Storage, Preparation and Handling</h4>
<p>A product issue resulting from circumstances occurring after retail sale will not ordinarily constitute an Apex Feeds manufacturing defect. This may include deterioration resulting from inappropriate customer storage, excessive heat, moisture exposure, contamination after opening, incorrect preparation, failure to follow applicable storage instructions or other customer-controlled circumstances. The existence of one of these circumstances should not simply be assumed. Where the cause is uncertain, the Retailer should obtain reasonable information and allow the issue to be properly assessed.</p>
<h4 id="s20-10">20.10 Animal Refusal or Preference</h4>
<p>An animal refusing to consume an otherwise suitable Apex Feeds product does not, by itself, establish that the product is defective. Individual animals may differ in feeding behaviour, preferences and acceptance of particular foods. Retailers may provide reasonable feeding or husbandry guidance in accordance with Section 12 but must not make unsupported guarantees regarding product acceptance or feeding outcomes.</p>
<h4 id="s20-11">20.11 Customer Returns to Saleable Inventory</h4>
<p>Food, feed, supplements or other consumable products that have been opened, used, prepared, contaminated or materially removed from their original condition after sale must not be returned to normal saleable inventory as new product. A factory-sealed product returned unopened may only be returned to saleable inventory where the Retailer is reasonably satisfied that its packaging, storage history, product integrity and applicable legal requirements make doing so appropriate.</p>
<p>Where the Retailer cannot reasonably establish that a returned consumable product has been appropriately stored and remains suitable for sale, it should not be returned to saleable inventory.</p>
<h4 id="s20-12">20.12 Retail Purchases Made Directly From Apex Feeds</h4>
<p>Where a customer purchased the product directly from Apex Feeds rather than through an Authorised Retailer, the customer should be directed to Apex Feeds for management of that transaction. The Retailer is not required to refund or replace a product purchased directly from Apex Feeds unless the Retailer voluntarily chooses to assist.</p>
<h4 id="s20-13">20.13 Customer Purchases From Another Retailer</h4>
<p>An Authorised Retailer is not required to provide a refund, replacement or other remedy for an Apex Feeds product purchased from another independent Retailer. The customer should ordinarily be referred to the business from which the product was purchased. This does not prevent a Retailer from assisting the customer with product information or directing a genuine product-safety concern to Apex Feeds.</p>
<h4 id="s20-14">20.14 Apex Feeds Support</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds will reasonably assist Authorised Retailers in investigating genuine complaints involving potential manufacturing defects, contamination, packaging failures, batch concerns or other product-quality issues. Where a valid Apex Feeds claim is established, the financial or stock adjustment between Apex Feeds and the Retailer will be dealt with under the applicable wholesale claims provisions of this Agreement.</p>
<h4 id="s20-15">20.15 Consumer Law</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Agreement is intended to exclude, restrict or modify any consumer guarantee, right, remedy or other obligation that cannot lawfully be excluded, restricted or modified under the Australian Consumer Law or other applicable law. Where this Agreement and a non-excludable legal obligation are inconsistent, the applicable legal obligation prevails to the extent of the inconsistency.</p>
<h3 id="s21">21. Retailer Claims to Apex Feeds</h3>
<p>Where an Authorised Retailer identifies a potential manufacturing defect, product-quality issue, packing error, freight-related issue or other circumstance that may reasonably give rise to a claim against Apex Feeds, the Retailer must provide sufficient information to allow the claim to be fairly assessed.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds will investigate genuine claims reasonably and, where a valid claim is established, provide an appropriate remedy having regard to the nature of the issue, the affected product and applicable law. Unless otherwise required by law or expressly agreed by Apex Feeds, a remedy provided to the Retailer is based upon the Retailer’s actual wholesale transaction with Apex Feeds, rather than the Retailer’s recommended or actual retail selling price.</p>
<h4 id="s21-1">21.1 Who May Submit a Wholesale Claim</h4>
<p>Wholesale claims should be submitted by the registered wholesale account owner or an authorised representative of the Retailer. Where multiple approved locations operate under the same legal entity and wholesale account, claims may be submitted in relation to stock supplied to any approved location covered by that account. A customer’s complaint to Apex Feeds does not automatically constitute a wholesale claim by the Retailer.</p>
<h4 id="s21-2">21.2 Information Required</h4>
<p> Depending on the nature of the claim, Apex Feeds may reasonably require:</p>
<ul>
<li>Retailer name and wholesale account details;</li>
<li>applicable invoice or order number;</li>
<li>product name and quantity affected;</li>
<li>Batch Number (BN);</li>
<li>Best Before Date (BBD);</li>
<li>photographs or video of the affected product;</li>
<li>photographs of packaging or freight cartons where relevant;</li>
<li>date the issue was identified;</li>
<li>a description of the alleged issue;</li>
<li>relevant storage, handling or temperature information;</li>
<li>information supplied by the Retailer’s customer where the claim originated from a customer complaint; and</li>
<li>other information reasonably necessary to determine the cause and appropriate remedy.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apex Feeds will not require information unrelated to the genuine assessment of the claim.</p>
<h4 id="s21-3">21.3 Reporting Claims Promptly</h4>
<p>Retailers must report potential claims within the specific reporting period applicable to that type of claim where one is established under this Agreement. This includes the 24-hour live feeder arrival period under Section 17 and applicable freight-reporting requirements elsewhere in this Agreement.</p>
<p>For manufacturing or product-quality concerns that may not reasonably be discoverable immediately, the Retailer should report the issue as soon as reasonably practicable after discovery.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds may take unreasonable delay into account where the delay materially affects its ability to establish the cause, condition or circumstances of the alleged issue.</p>
<h4 id="s21-4">21.4 Quarantine and Preservation of Evidence</h4>
<p>Where potentially affected stock remains in the Retailer’s possession, the Retailer must follow the applicable quarantine and evidence-preservation requirements of Section 19. Apex Feeds may reasonably request that affected stock or packaging be retained while the claim is assessed.</p>
<p>The Retailer should not dispose of material evidence relevant to a claim before Apex Feeds has had a reasonable opportunity to assess it, except where immediate disposal is reasonably necessary for health, safety, contamination, biosecurity or legal reasons.</p>
<h4 id="s21-5">21.5 Assessment of Claims</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may consider all reasonably relevant circumstances when assessing a claim, including:</p>
<ul>
<li>product and Batch Number;</li>
<li>nature and consistency of the alleged issue;</li>
<li>photographs, video and physical evidence;</li>
<li>condition of other products from the same batch;</li>
<li>production and quality-control information;</li>
<li>storage and handling history;</li>
<li>freight circumstances;</li>
<li>temperature exposure where relevant;</li>
<li>time between supply, discovery and reporting;</li>
<li>whether the product was opened or prepared;</li>
<li>whether the issue appears isolated or repeated; and</li>
<li>any other information reasonably relevant to establishing the likely cause.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apex Feeds will not unreasonably reject a claim merely because every possible piece of evidence is unavailable where the available information is otherwise sufficient to reasonably establish the issue.</p>
<h4 id="s21-6">21.6 Valid Apex Feeds Claims</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds reasonably determines that a claim relates to a defect, packing error, product-quality failure or other matter for which Apex Feeds is responsible, Apex Feeds may provide an appropriate remedy including:</p>
<ul>
<li>replacement of affected stock;</li>
<li>wholesale account credit;</li>
<li>refund of the applicable wholesale product value;</li>
<li>correction of a shortage or incorrect supply; or</li>
<li>another remedy reasonably appropriate to the circumstances.</li>
</ul>
<p>The remedy selected will take into account the nature of the issue, availability of replacement stock, the Retailer’s circumstances and any applicable legal requirements.</p>
<h4 id="s21-7">21.7 Basis of Wholesale Credit or Refund</h4>
<p>Unless otherwise required by law or expressly agreed by Apex Feeds, any wholesale credit or refund will be calculated using the actual amount paid or payable by the Retailer to Apex Feeds for the affected product, taking into account any applicable wholesale discount, promotion or other adjustment applying to that transaction.</p>
<p>The Retailer’s recommended retail price, actual selling price or normal retail margin does not determine the amount of the wholesale product credit.</p>
<p>For example, where a Retailer purchased an affected product from Apex Feeds for $10.00 excluding GST and ordinarily retails that product for $19.95, the product-value component of the wholesale claim is based on the Retailer’s $10.00 wholesale purchase rather than the $19.95 retail selling price, subject to applicable law and any additional remedy Apex Feeds expressly agrees to provide.</p>
<h4 id="s21-8">21.8 Customer Refunds Do Not Automatically Determine Apex Liability</h4>
<p>A Retailer may determine that refunding or replacing a customer’s product immediately is appropriate for customer service or consumer-law purposes. The Retailer does not need to delay appropriate customer service merely because Apex Feeds has not yet completed its wholesale claim assessment.</p>
<p>However, the Retailer’s decision to refund, replace or otherwise compensate its customer does not automatically establish that Apex Feeds is responsible for the issue or determine the amount of any subsequent wholesale credit.</p>
<p>The corresponding claim between the Retailer and Apex Feeds will be assessed independently under this Agreement.</p>
<h4 id="s21-9">21.9 Retailer Margin and Consequential Commercial Loss</h4>
<p>Unless required by applicable law or expressly accepted by Apex Feeds in writing, an ordinary product claim does not entitle the Retailer to reimbursement for anticipated retail profit, lost margin, staff time, administrative costs, loss of future sales or other indirect commercial loss merely because affected stock could not be sold. This clause does not limit any liability that cannot lawfully be limited or excluded.</p>
<h4 id="s21-10">21.10 Freight Charges</h4>
<p>The treatment of freight charges associated with damaged, lost, incorrectly supplied or defective products will depend upon the circumstances of the claim and the applicable requirements of this Agreement. A valid claim concerning an individual product does not automatically entitle the Retailer to reimbursement of the freight charged for an otherwise successfully delivered wholesale order.</p>
<p>Where additional freight is reasonably necessary to provide a remedy for an issue for which Apex Feeds is responsible, Apex Feeds will determine appropriate freight arrangements having regard to the circumstances and applicable law. Freight damage and carrier-related claims are addressed further in Section 23.</p>
<h4 id="s21-11">21.11 Physical Return of Affected Stock</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may require physical return of affected stock where reasonably necessary to investigate a claim, confirm the alleged issue, undertake quality-control assessment or prevent inappropriate continued use or sale.</p>
<p>Where Apex Feeds requires the physical return of stock in connection with a claim that appears reasonably capable of being valid, Apex Feeds will provide or agree appropriate return arrangements. Apex Feeds will not require physical return where doing so would be unsafe, unreasonable or disproportionate to the value or nature of the claim.</p>
<h4 id="s21-12">21.12 Replacement Stock</h4>
<p>Where replacement stock is provided, the replacement ordinarily satisfies the product-value component of the accepted claim unless Apex Feeds agrees otherwise or applicable law requires an additional remedy. Replacement stock will be subject to availability. Where the same product is unavailable, Apex Feeds and the Retailer may agree upon an account credit, refund or suitable alternative.</p>
<h4 id="s21-13">21.13 Account Credits</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds issues a wholesale account credit, the credit will be recorded against the Retailer’s wholesale account and may be applied against an existing amount owing or a future wholesale order, as applicable. Where the circumstances or applicable law make an account credit inappropriate, Apex Feeds may instead provide a refund or other suitable remedy.</p>
<h4 id="s21-14">21.14 Repeated or Batch-Related Claims</h4>
<p>Where multiple similar claims arise concerning the same product or Batch Number, Apex Feeds may consolidate the investigation and initiate the traceability procedures under Section 26 or product withdrawal and recall procedures under Section 27 where appropriate. Retailers must reasonably cooperate with requests to identify affected quantities and remaining stock.</p>
<h4 id="s21-15">21.15 Fraudulent or Misleading Claims</h4>
<p>Retailers must not knowingly provide false, altered, fabricated or materially misleading information or evidence in support of a wholesale claim. A genuine mistake or reasonable disagreement concerning the cause or condition of a product does not constitute a fraudulent claim.</p>
<p>Where Apex Feeds has reasonable grounds to believe that claim information has been deliberately falsified, it may investigate the matter and take proportionate action under the breach provisions of this Agreement.</p>
<h4 id="s21-16">21.16 Claim Records</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may maintain reasonable records of wholesale claims, including the product, Batch Number, nature of the issue, evidence supplied, investigation outcome and remedy provided. Such records may be used for quality control, manufacturing review, traceability, identification of recurring issues and administration of the wholesale relationship.</p>
<h4 id="s21-17">21.17 No Admission From Investigation or Goodwill Remedy</h4>
<p>The investigation of a claim, collection of affected stock, provision of technical assistance or provision of a discretionary goodwill remedy does not, by itself, constitute an admission by Apex Feeds that the product was defective or that Apex Feeds was legally responsible for the alleged issue.</p>
<h4 id="s21-18">21.18 Non-Excludable Rights</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section excludes, restricts or modifies any right, remedy, guarantee or liability that cannot lawfully be excluded, restricted or modified.</p>
<h3 id="s22">22. Manufacturing Defects</h3>
<p>Apex Feeds is responsible for maintaining reasonable manufacturing, preparation, packaging and quality-control standards for products it manufactures or packages. Where an Apex Feeds product contains a genuine manufacturing, formulation, contamination, packaging or other quality defect attributable to Apex Feeds, the affected product will be assessed and remedied in accordance with Sections 19–21 and applicable law.</p>
<p>Not every difference in appearance, texture, aroma, ingredient distribution or animal acceptance constitutes a manufacturing defect. Apex Feeds products may contain natural ingredients that reasonably vary between production batches.</p>
<h4 id="s22-1">22.1 Examples of Potential Manufacturing Defects</h4>
<p>Depending upon the circumstances, a manufacturing or product-quality defect may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>mould, spoilage or deterioration present despite appropriate storage and handling;</li>
<li>foreign material not reasonably expected to form part of the product;</li>
<li>contamination arising during Apex Feeds’ manufacturing, processing or packaging;</li>
<li>incorrect formulation or significant manufacturing error;</li>
<li>incorrect product packed into labelled packaging;</li>
<li>material packaging or seal failure attributable to manufacturing or packing;</li>
<li>missing or materially incorrect mandatory product information;</li>
<li>significant deviation from the intended product where the deviation affects its suitability, quality or represented characteristics;</li>
<li>incorrect Batch Number or Best Before Date information attributable to Apex Feeds;</li>
<li>significant underfilling or incorrect quantity attributable to Apex Feeds; or</li>
<li>another manufacturing or packaging failure that means the product does not reasonably conform to the product supplied or represented by Apex Feeds.</li>
</ul>
<p>The existence of one of these circumstances will be assessed according to the available evidence and does not need to be presumed merely from a customer allegation.</p>
<h4 id="s22-2">22.2 Natural Ingredient Variation</h4>
<p>Many Apex Feeds products are manufactured using natural animal, insect, plant and other food or feed ingredients. Natural variation may occur between ingredient supplies and production batches.</p>
<p>Accordingly, reasonable differences may occur in:</p>
<ul>
<li>colour or colour intensity;</li>
<li>aroma;</li>
<li>texture;</li>
<li>particle size;</li>
<li>visible ingredient size or distribution;</li>
<li>moisture appearance within the normal characteristics of the product;</li>
<li>proportions of individually visible ingredients within a particular serving;</li>
<li>natural shape or appearance of whole or partially processed ingredients; and</li>
<li>other minor sensory or visual characteristics arising from natural ingredients.</li>
</ul>
<p>Reasonable natural variation that does not materially affect the product’s identity, quality, safety or represented characteristics does not, by itself, constitute a manufacturing defect.</p>
<h4 id="s22-3">22.3 Visible Ingredients and Distribution</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds products may contain visible whole, diced, dehydrated, freeze-dried, powdered, minced or otherwise processed ingredients. Individual packages or servings are not guaranteed to contain an identical visual distribution of every visible ingredient unless Apex Feeds expressly states otherwise.</p>
<p>Reasonable settling, movement or redistribution of ingredients during manufacturing, packaging, freight or storage does not automatically constitute a defect. Where appropriate, customers may be instructed to mix or otherwise prepare a product in accordance with its applicable preparation instructions.</p>
<h4 id="s22-4">22.4 Colour and Appearance</h4>
<p>Differences in natural ingredient colour between batches do not automatically indicate deterioration, incorrect formulation or reduced nutritional value. Seasonal variation, ingredient supply, processing, dehydration, freeze-drying and other normal production factors may affect the final appearance of a product. However, unusual discolouration accompanied by mould, abnormal odour, packaging failure, moisture exposure or another indication of deterioration should be treated as a potential quality concern under Section 19.</p>
<h4 id="s22-5">22.5 Aroma</h4>
<p>Natural ingredients may produce variations in aroma between products or batches. A stronger, weaker or somewhat different natural food aroma does not, by itself, establish that a product is defective. An abnormal or unexpected odour reasonably suggesting spoilage, contamination or deterioration should be reported and assessed as a potential product-quality concern.</p>
<h4 id="s22-6">22.6 Product Form and Texture</h4>
<p>Reasonable differences in firmness, brittleness, crumbling, powder distribution, ingredient size or other physical characteristics may occur as a result of the ingredients and manufacturing processes used. Minor breakage or movement occurring during normal freight and handling does not automatically constitute a manufacturing defect where the product remains otherwise suitable and materially consistent with its intended use. A substantial or abnormal difference that prevents the product from being prepared or used as intended may be submitted for assessment.</p>
<h4 id="s22-7">22.7 Packaging Defects</h4>
<p>Packaging will be considered in determining whether a product-quality issue is attributable to Apex Feeds. A potential Apex Feeds packaging defect may include an improperly formed or failed factory seal, materially incorrect packaging, manufacturing-related puncture or another packaging failure existing when the product left Apex Feeds’ control. Damage occurring after dispatch through freight is addressed under Section 23.</p>
<p>Damage occurring after successful delivery through Retailer or customer handling is not ordinarily a manufacturing defect.</p>
<h4 id="s22-8">22.8 Best Before Date and Batch Number</h4>
<p>A missing, incorrect or improperly applied Best Before Date or Batch Number attributable to Apex Feeds may constitute a packaging or identification issue requiring correction. Where Apex Feeds can reliably establish the correct information, Apex Feeds may provide authorised replacement identification or other appropriate instructions. Retailers must not independently estimate or recreate missing Batch Number or Best Before information.</p>
<h4 id="s22-9">22.9 Incorrect Formulation or Product</h4>
<p>Where there are reasonable grounds to believe that a product has been manufactured using an incorrect formulation, incorrect ingredient, materially incorrect quantity of an ingredient or has been packed as the wrong product, the affected stock must be quarantined and Apex Feeds notified promptly. Apex Feeds may investigate relevant production and Batch records and may expand the investigation to other stock from the same Batch Number where appropriate.</p>
<h4 id="s22-10">22.10 Customer or Animal Acceptance</h4>
<p>An animal refusing to eat, showing reduced interest in, or preferring another product does not, by itself, establish a manufacturing defect. Individual animal feeding behaviour and preferences can vary considerably. Similarly, a customer disliking the appearance, smell, texture, preparation method or other characteristic of an otherwise conforming product does not, by itself, establish that the product is defective. This does not prevent investigation where the reported behaviour accompanies reasonable evidence of an actual product-quality concern.</p>
<h4 id="s22-11">22.11 Preparation and Rehydration</h4>
<p>Where a product requires rehydration, mixing, thawing or another form of preparation before use, the result may depend upon the preparation method used. A result caused by materially incorrect preparation, incorrect water quantity, inappropriate temperature, substitution of preparation instructions or other customer or Retailer-controlled preparation error does not ordinarily constitute a manufacturing defect. Where the product fails to prepare or perform substantially as intended despite reasonable compliance with Apex Feeds’ instructions, the matter may be submitted for assessment.</p>
<h4 id="s22-12">22.12 Storage and Handling After Supply</h4>
<p>Deterioration caused by inappropriate storage or handling after successful delivery does not ordinarily constitute a manufacturing defect. This includes circumstances addressed under Sections 14–19, such as excessive heat, moisture, contamination, freezer failure, inappropriate thawing, pest exposure or other conditions arising after the product entered the Retailer’s control. Where the cause is genuinely uncertain, Apex Feeds and the Retailer should assess the available evidence rather than automatically attributing the issue to either party.</p>
<h4 id="s22-13">22.13 Isolated Defect Versus Batch Concern</h4>
<p>A defect affecting an individual product does not automatically establish that all products carrying the same Batch Number are defective.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds may assess whether the circumstances indicate:</p>
<ul>
<li>an isolated packaging or product issue;</li>
<li>damage occurring after manufacture;</li>
<li>a limited production issue;</li>
<li>a broader Batch-related concern; or</li>
<li>another identifiable cause.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where evidence reasonably indicates that additional stock may be affected, Apex Feeds may initiate the traceability, quarantine, withdrawal or recall procedures contained elsewhere in this Agreement.</p>
<h4 id="s22-14">22.14 Product Improvements and Manufacturing Changes</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may reasonably improve or modify manufacturing processes, ingredient sourcing, processing methods, equipment, packaging or other production characteristics over time provided the resulting product remains consistent with its applicable product identity, representations and legal requirements.</p>
<p>A reasonable manufacturing improvement or change does not constitute a defect merely because the product differs slightly from an earlier production batch. Where a change materially affects product use, preparation, storage, ingredients or other information reasonably important to the Retailer or customer, Apex Feeds will update applicable product information as appropriate.</p>
<h4 id="s22-15">22.15 Investigation and Remedy</h4>
<p>Potential manufacturing defects will be investigated in accordance with Sections 19 and 21. Where a genuine manufacturing defect attributable to Apex Feeds is established, Apex Feeds will provide an appropriate remedy having regard to the affected stock, circumstances and applicable law.</p>
<p>Where investigation indicates that the product remains suitable and the reported characteristic represents normal product variation rather than a defect, Apex Feeds may explain the basis of that determination to the Retailer and provide supporting product information where appropriate.</p>
<h4 id="s22-16">22.16 Non-Excludable Rights</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section excludes, restricts or modifies any guarantee, right, remedy or liability that cannot lawfully be excluded, restricted or modified.</p>
<h3 id="s23">23. Freight Damage</h3>
<p>Apex Feeds takes reasonable steps to package wholesale orders appropriately for the products being supplied and the expected method of transport. Where products are damaged, lost or materially compromised during freight between Apex Feeds and the Retailer’s nominated delivery location, the Retailer must inspect, document and report the issue in accordance with this Section and the applicable delivery requirements under Section 7.</p>
<p>Freight damage will be assessed according to the condition of the affected products and available evidence rather than the condition of the external shipping carton alone.</p>
<h4 id="s23-1">23.1 Inspection Following Delivery</h4>
<p>Wholesale deliveries should be inspected as soon as reasonably practicable following receipt.</p>
<p>Retailers should check for obvious:</p>
<ul>
<li>carton crushing, punctures or significant impact damage;</li>
<li>water or liquid exposure;</li>
<li>opened or materially damaged shipping packaging;</li>
<li>damaged, punctured, split or leaking product packaging;</li>
<li>missing products or quantities;</li>
<li>evidence of significant frozen-product temperature exposure;</li>
<li>abnormal live feeder mortality or shipping-container failure; and</li>
<li>other circumstances reasonably indicating that products may have been damaged or compromised during freight.</li>
</ul>
<p>Products requiring frozen or specialised storage should be transferred into appropriate storage promptly while the shipment is being assessed.</p>
<h4 id="s23-2">23.2 Reporting Visible Freight Damage</h4>
<p>Visible freight damage affecting standard wholesale products should be reported to Apex Feeds within 3 days of receiving the order. The Retailer should provide reasonable supporting information including the affected product, quantity, order or invoice details and photographs showing the damage.</p>
<p>Where possible, photographs should show both the affected products and relevant external shipping packaging. A delay in reporting may be taken into account where it materially affects Apex Feeds’ or the carrier’s ability to determine when or how the damage occurred. Nothing in this clause excludes any right or remedy that cannot lawfully be excluded.</p>
<h4 id="s23-3">23.3 Live Feeder Freight Claims</h4>
<p>Claims concerning the condition or abnormal mortality of live feeders on arrival are subject to the 24-hour live-arrival claim period under Section 17. The general three-day freight reporting period does not extend the applicable live feeder claim period.</p>
<h4 id="s23-4">23.4 Frozen Product Freight Concerns</h4>
<p>Frozen products must be inspected and transferred to appropriate frozen storage as soon as reasonably practicable following delivery. Partial softening or partial thawing does not, by itself, constitute freight damage or product failure where the product has remained within the acceptable conditions established under Section 16.</p>
<p>Where there are reasonable grounds to believe frozen stock has experienced unacceptable temperature exposure, the Retailer should document the condition promptly and contact Apex Feeds. Affected products must be handled in accordance with Section 16 and, where necessary, quarantined under Section 19.</p>
<h4 id="s23-5">23.5 External Carton Damage</h4>
<p>Damage to an external shipping carton does not automatically establish that the products contained within it are damaged. Where the internal products remain sealed, correctly identified, uncontaminated and otherwise suitable for normal retail sale, cosmetic damage to the external freight carton alone will not ordinarily constitute a product-value claim.</p>
<h4 id="s23-6">23.6 Cosmetic Retail Packaging Damage</h4>
<p>Minor cosmetic damage to retail packaging does not automatically make a product defective or unsaleable. Where freight damage materially affects the retail presentation, integrity, seal, required information or reasonable saleability of an individual product, the affected product may be submitted as part of the freight claim. Apex Feeds will assess the actual extent of the damage rather than requiring a product to be physically unsafe before a claim can be considered.</p>
<h4 id="s23-7">23.7 Partial Order Damage</h4>
<p>Damage affecting part of a wholesale shipment does not automatically make the entire order subject to a claim. Where only particular products or quantities have been damaged or compromised, the claim and applicable remedy will ordinarily relate to those affected products or quantities. Unaffected products should remain in normal inventory where they are otherwise suitable for sale.</p>
<h4 id="s23-8">23.8 Preservation of Freight Evidence</h4>
<p>Where a freight claim is being made, the Retailer should retain reasonable evidence of the condition in which the shipment was received.</p>
<p>Depending upon the circumstances, this may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>photographs or video of the shipping carton before disposal;</li>
<li>shipping labels;</li>
<li>damaged internal packaging;</li>
<li>affected products;</li>
<li>insulation or frozen-shipping materials where relevant;</li>
<li>Batch Numbers and Best Before Dates; and</li>
<li>other evidence reasonably relevant to the freight issue.</li>
</ul>
<p>Retailers are not expected to indefinitely retain bulky freight packaging once sufficient evidence has been captured and Apex Feeds has confirmed that physical retention is unnecessary.</p>
<h4 id="s23-9">23.9 Carrier Investigation</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may lodge or pursue an investigation or claim with the applicable freight carrier where appropriate. The Retailer must provide reasonable information or evidence necessary to assist with that process. The existence or outcome of a carrier investigation does not prevent Apex Feeds from dealing appropriately with the Retailer’s claim where Apex Feeds has an obligation to do so.</p>
<h4 id="s23-10">23.10 Lost or Missing Shipments</h4>
<p>A shipment that has not arrived within its estimated delivery period is not automatically considered lost. Where tracking indicates a delay, missing scan or other delivery issue, Apex Feeds may make reasonable enquiries with the carrier and allow a reasonable period for the shipment to be located or delivered.</p>
<p>Where the carrier confirms the shipment has been lost, or the circumstances reasonably establish that it is unlikely to be delivered, Apex Feeds will determine the appropriate replacement, credit, refund or other remedy having regard to the order and applicable law.</p>
<h4 id="s23-11">23.11 Incorrect or Missing Products</h4>
<p>Products missing from a successfully delivered shipment, incorrect quantities or products incorrectly packed by Apex Feeds are not freight damage but may be reported through the same initial order-reporting process. As established under Section 4, visible shortages, incorrect products, incorrect quantities or picking errors should be reported within 3 days of receiving the order. Where Apex Feeds confirms a packing or supply error, an appropriate correction will be made.</p>
<h4 id="s23-12">23.12 Damage Caused After Delivery</h4>
<p>Damage occurring after successful delivery as a result of Retailer handling, storage, equipment, internal transport or other circumstances under the Retailer’s control does not constitute freight damage. Where the timing or cause of damage is genuinely uncertain, Apex Feeds and the Retailer should assess the available evidence reasonably rather than automatically attributing responsibility to either party.</p>
<h4 id="s23-13">23.13 Unattended or Failed Delivery</h4>
<p>Where damage or deterioration results materially from the Retailer’s failure to provide an accurate delivery address, accept an expected delivery, provide reasonable access or appropriately receive temperature-sensitive stock, the resulting loss will not ordinarily constitute a freight claim against Apex Feeds.</p>
<p>This does not apply where the carrier failed to follow the applicable delivery arrangement or the circumstances were otherwise outside the Retailer’s reasonable control.</p>
<h4 id="s23-14">23.14 Remedy for Valid Freight Damage</h4>
<p>Where a valid freight-damage claim is established, the affected products may be replaced, credited, refunded or otherwise remedied in accordance with Section 21 and applicable law. Any product-value credit or refund will ordinarily be based upon the Retailer’s applicable wholesale purchase value rather than the product’s recommended or actual retail selling price. Where replacement stock is supplied, Apex Feeds will determine reasonable freight arrangements having regard to the nature and extent of the original freight failure.</p>
<h4 id="s23-15">23.15 No Double Recovery</h4>
<p>Where the Retailer has already received replacement, credit, refund or other compensation for the same affected stock, the Retailer is not entitled to receive duplicate compensation for that same loss. Where Apex Feeds manages a carrier claim relating to stock for which Apex Feeds has already remedied the Retailer, any carrier reimbursement relating to that same loss may be retained by Apex Feeds.</p>
<h4 id="s23-16">23.16 Non-Excludable Rights</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section excludes, restricts or modifies any right, remedy, guarantee or liability that cannot lawfully be excluded, restricted or modified.</p>
<h3 id="s24">24. Excluded Claims</h3>
<p>Apex Feeds will reasonably assess genuine wholesale claims relating to manufacturing defects, product-quality failures, packing errors, valid freight issues and other matters for which Apex Feeds is responsible.</p>
<p>However, not every product loss, customer return, deterioration, animal refusal, expired product or commercial loss experienced by a Retailer constitutes a valid claim against Apex Feeds. Subject to applicable law, the circumstances set out in this Section will not ordinarily entitle the Retailer to replacement, refund, wholesale credit or other compensation from Apex Feeds where the relevant loss was not caused by Apex Feeds.</p>
<h4 id="s24-1">24.1 Change of Mind and Customer Preference</h4>
<p>A claim will not ordinarily be accepted merely because the Retailer or its customer: * changed their mind; * purchased or ordered the wrong product; * purchased more product than required; * no longer requires the product; * prefers another product, formulation, flavour, texture or feeding method; or * is otherwise dissatisfied for reasons unrelated to a defect or failure of the product to conform to its applicable description. Retailers remain free to provide discretionary customer-service remedies at their own cost in accordance with Section 20.</p>
<h4 id="s24-2">24.2 Animal Refusal or Individual Feeding Preference</h4>
<p>An animal refusing to consume an otherwise suitable Apex Feeds product does not, by itself, establish a product defect. Individual animals may differ in feeding behaviour, appetite, preferences and acceptance of particular foods. A claim may still be investigated where animal refusal accompanies reasonable evidence of an actual product-quality or safety concern.</p>
<h4 id="s24-3">24.3 Incorrect Preparation or Use</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds is not ordinarily responsible for product loss or unsatisfactory results caused by materially incorrect preparation or use after supply.</p>
<p>This may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>incorrect water quantities;</li>
<li>inappropriate preparation temperatures;</li>
<li>failure to follow material preparation instructions;</li>
<li>incorrect thawing or storage;</li>
<li>contamination during preparation;</li>
<li>addition of unsuitable ingredients;</li>
<li>inappropriate storage after preparation; or</li>
<li>use materially inconsistent with applicable product instructions.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where a product fails to prepare or perform substantially as intended despite reasonable compliance with Apex Feeds’ instructions, the matter may still be submitted for assessment.</p>
<h4 id="s24-4">24.4 Retailer Storage and Handling</h4>
<p>Loss, deterioration or damage caused after successful delivery by inappropriate Retailer storage, handling or equipment will not ordinarily constitute a valid claim against Apex Feeds.</p>
<p>This includes damage arising from:</p>
<ul>
<li>excessive heat;</li>
<li>moisture or water exposure;</li>
<li>inappropriate humidity;</li>
<li>freezer or refrigeration failure;</li>
<li>unacceptable frozen-product temperature exposure;</li>
<li>chemical contamination;</li>
<li>pest exposure;</li>
<li>unsanitary storage conditions;</li>
<li>mishandling;</li>
<li>accidental puncture, crushing or other physical damage; or</li>
<li>failure to follow applicable storage and handling requirements.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where the cause is genuinely uncertain, Apex Feeds and the Retailer should assess the available evidence reasonably rather than automatically attributing responsibility to either party.</p>
<h4 id="s24-5">24.5 Customer Storage and Handling</h4>
<p>A product issue caused after retail sale by the customer’s storage, transport, preparation, handling or contamination will not ordinarily constitute a manufacturing defect or wholesale claim against Apex Feeds. This does not prevent investigation where there is reasonable evidence that the product was already defective or compromised before it entered the customer’s control.</p>
<h4 id="s24-6">24.6 Retailer Freezer or Equipment Failure</h4>
<p>Stock loss resulting from failure, malfunction, disconnection or inappropriate operation of the Retailer’s freezer, refrigeration, heating, environmental-control, storage or other equipment will not ordinarily be the responsibility of Apex Feeds. Frozen stock affected by freezer failure must be assessed in accordance with Section 16. The fact that stock requires disposal following a Retailer equipment failure does not, by itself, create an entitlement to replacement or wholesale credit.</p>
<h4 id="s24-7">24.7 Live Feeder Husbandry Losses</h4>
<p>Following successful delivery and expiry of the applicable 24-hour live-arrival claim period, ordinary deterioration or mortality of live feeders resulting from subsequent husbandry or environmental conditions under the Retailer’s control will not ordinarily constitute a claim against Apex Feeds. This may include losses associated with inappropriate temperature, ventilation, hydration, feeding, stocking conditions, contamination, containment or other post-delivery husbandry circumstances.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds may still provide reasonable husbandry assistance under Section 17 even where the resulting stock loss is not an Apex Feeds claim.</p>
<h4 id="s24-8">24.8 Normal Live Feeder Mortality</h4>
<p>Reasonable mortality associated with holding living feeder insects does not automatically constitute a defect or continuing guarantee of live stock after delivery. Nothing in this clause limits a valid live-arrival claim made in accordance with Section 17.</p>
<h4 id="s24-9">24.9 Natural Product Variation</h4>
<p>Reasonable natural variation of the kind described in Section 22 does not constitute a manufacturing defect merely because one production batch differs visually or physically from another. This may include reasonable variation in colour, aroma, texture, ingredient appearance, particle size, visible ingredient distribution or other natural characteristics. A materially abnormal variation affecting product identity, quality, safety or intended use may still be submitted for assessment.</p>
<h4 id="s24-10">24.10 Normal Settling, Movement or Minor Breakage</h4>
<p>Reasonable settling, redistribution, crumbling or movement of ingredients during ordinary manufacturing, freight and storage does not automatically constitute a product defect. Minor physical changes that do not materially affect product quality, safety, use or reasonable retail presentation will not ordinarily support a wholesale claim.</p>
<h4 id="s24-11">24.11 Cosmetic Freight or Packaging Damage</h4>
<p>Minor cosmetic damage to an external shipping carton or retail packaging does not automatically constitute a valid claim where the actual product remains sealed, identifiable, uncontaminated and reasonably suitable for retail sale. Material freight damage affecting product integrity or reasonable retail saleability remains subject to Section 23.</p>
<h4 id="s24-12">24.12 Short-Dated and Expired Retailer Stock</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds is not ordinarily responsible for stock becoming short-dated or reaching its Best Before Date while held in the Retailer’s inventory where the product was originally supplied with an appropriate remaining saleable life. Loss caused by Retailer ordering quantities, low turnover or failure to reasonably rotate stock will ordinarily remain the Retailer’s inventory risk. This does not apply where Apex Feeds failed to disclose a material short-dated condition that should reasonably have been disclosed or failed to meet an expressly agreed shelf-life requirement.</p>
<h4 id="s24-13">24.13 Disclosed Clearance or Short-Dated Purchases</h4>
<p>Stock purchased with prior disclosure that it is short-dated, clearance, discontinued or otherwise subject to a disclosed condition will not be considered defective merely because of that disclosed circumstance. Any genuine unrelated defect remains capable of being claimed in the ordinary manner.</p>
<h4 id="s24-14">24.14 Unauthorised Repackaging or Alteration</h4>
<p>Except for the permitted repacking of live feeder insects under Section 17, Apex Feeds is not ordinarily responsible for product loss or deterioration arising after a Retailer independently:</p>
<ul>
<li>opens or divides bulk manufactured product;</li>
<li>repackages product;</li>
<li>alters the original packaging;</li>
<li>relabels product;</li>
<li>reformulates or mixes product with other materials;</li>
<li>removes or alters storage or preparation information; or</li>
<li>otherwise materially changes the product from the condition in which Apex Feeds supplied it, unless that activity was specifically authorised by Apex Feeds and carried out in accordance with the applicable authorised process.</li>
</ul>
<p>This clause does not affect genuine defects that existed before the Retailer’s alteration and were unrelated to that alteration.</p>
<h4 id="s24-15">24.15 Unauthorised Product Claims or Representations</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds is not ordinarily responsible for a loss arising from a Retailer’s own inaccurate, misleading, unauthorised or unsupported representation about an Apex Feeds product where that representation was not supplied or authorised by Apex Feeds.</p>
<p>This includes an independent guarantee or representation concerning animal acceptance, performance, health outcomes, nutritional characteristics or product use that materially exceeds Apex Feeds’ applicable product information. This clause does not apply where the Retailer accurately relied upon information supplied or authorised by Apex Feeds.</p>
<h4 id="s24-16">24.16 Retailer Pricing and Commercial Decisions</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds is not responsible for losses arising solely from the Retailer’s independent retail pricing, discounts, promotions, clearance decisions, purchasing quantities or other ordinary commercial decisions. The fact that a Retailer subsequently sells stock below its normal retail price does not create a claim against Apex Feeds.</p>
<h4 id="s24-17">24.17 Lost Retail Margin and Indirect Commercial Loss</h4>
<p>Subject to applicable law and Section 21, an ordinary product claim does not automatically entitle the Retailer to compensation for:</p>
<ul>
<li>anticipated retail profit;</li>
<li>lost retail margin;</li>
<li>staff or administrative time;</li>
<li>loss of future sales;</li>
<li>general business interruption;</li>
<li>reputational loss; or</li>
<li>other indirect or consequential commercial loss.</li>
</ul>
<p>This does not exclude liability to the extent that it cannot lawfully be excluded or limited.</p>
<h4 id="s24-18">24.18 Failure to Preserve Reasonable Evidence</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may decline or reduce a claim where the Retailer’s unreasonable failure to retain or provide reasonably available evidence makes it materially impossible to establish whether a valid Apex Feeds issue occurred.</p>
<p>A claim will not be rejected merely because a particular photograph, package or item is unavailable where sufficient other evidence reasonably establishes the issue. Immediate disposal required for health, safety, contamination, biosecurity or legal reasons will be treated in accordance with Section 19.</p>
<h4 id="s24-19">24.19 Claims Outside an Applicable Reporting Period</h4>
<p>Where this Agreement establishes a specific reporting period for a particular claim type, Apex Feeds may take failure to report within that period into account when assessing the claim. This includes the 24-hour live feeder arrival period and applicable reporting requirements for visible freight or order discrepancies.</p>
<p>A late report does not remove any right or remedy that cannot lawfully be excluded and will not automatically defeat a claim where the nature of the issue means it could not reasonably have been identified earlier.</p>
<h4 id="s24-20">24.20 Fraudulent or Deliberately Misleading Claims</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds is not required to provide compensation for a claim supported by information or evidence that the Retailer knowingly fabricated, falsified or materially misrepresented. A genuine mistake, uncertainty regarding the cause of a problem or reasonable disagreement with Apex Feeds does not constitute fraud or deliberate misrepresentation.</p>
<h4 id="s24-21">24.21 Apex Feeds-Caused Issues Remain Covered</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section is intended to exclude a claim merely because one of the circumstances above exists where the available evidence reasonably establishes that an independent manufacturing, packing, freight or other Apex Feeds-related failure materially caused or contributed to the loss.</p>
<p>Where responsibility appears to be shared or the cause is genuinely uncertain, Apex Feeds will assess the circumstances reasonably having regard to the available evidence.</p>
<h4 id="s24-22">24.22 Non-Excludable Rights</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section excludes, restricts or modifies any guarantee, right, remedy, liability or obligation that cannot lawfully be excluded, restricted or modified. Where applicable law provides a non-excludable right or remedy, that law prevails to the extent of any inconsistency with this Agreement.</p>
<h3 id="s25">25. Product &amp; Animal Safety Complaints</h3>
<p>Apex Feeds treats genuine product-safety and animal-safety concerns seriously. Where an Authorised Retailer receives information suggesting that an Apex Feeds product may be associated with animal illness, injury, abnormal adverse effects, contamination, foreign material or another significant safety concern, the Retailer must take reasonable steps to preserve relevant information and notify Apex Feeds promptly.</p>
<p>The purpose of this Section is to allow potential safety issues to be identified, investigated and, where necessary, contained as quickly as reasonably practicable. The reporting or investigation of an adverse event does not, by itself, establish that an Apex Feeds product caused or contributed to the event.</p>
<h4 id="s25-1">25.1 Safety Complaints Requiring Prompt Escalation</h4>
<p>The Retailer should notify Apex Feeds as soon as reasonably practicable where a customer reports or the Retailer identifies a potentially serious concern involving:</p>
<ul>
<li>animal illness or injury allegedly associated with an Apex Feeds product;</li>
<li>death of an animal allegedly associated with an Apex Feeds product;</li>
<li>suspected contamination;</li>
<li>suspected foreign material;</li>
<li>mould, spoilage or abnormal deterioration potentially affecting product safety;</li>
<li>suspected incorrect formulation or ingredients;</li>
<li>unexpected material capable of creating an animal-safety risk;</li>
<li>multiple similar complaints involving the same product or Batch Number;</li>
<li>a significant packaging failure potentially affecting product integrity; or</li>
<li>another circumstance reasonably suggesting a potential product or animal-safety issue.</li>
</ul>
<p>Routine complaints involving animal preference, refusal to eat, minor natural product variation or other matters without a reasonable safety concern do not require emergency escalation under this Section.</p>
<h4 id="s25-2">25.2 Information to Obtain</h4>
<p>Where reasonably possible, the Retailer should obtain sufficient information to allow the complaint to be meaningfully investigated.</p>
<p>Relevant information may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>product name and variety;</li>
<li>Batch Number (BN);</li>
<li>Best Before Date (BBD);</li>
<li>date and location of purchase;</li>
<li>proof of purchase where available;</li>
<li>photographs or video of the product and packaging;</li>
<li>description of the reported animal reaction or safety concern;</li>
<li>approximate timing between feeding or product use and the reported event;</li>
<li>how the product was stored and prepared;</li>
<li>whether other animals consumed the same product and, if known, whether they experienced similar effects;</li>
<li>whether other foods, feeds, supplements or products were used around the same period; and</li>
<li>other information reasonably relevant to assessing the reported concern.</li>
</ul>
<p>Retailers are not expected to conduct their own veterinary investigation or establish the medical cause of an animal’s condition.</p>
<h4 id="s25-3">25.3 Preservation of Product and Packaging</h4>
<p>Where reasonably practicable, the Retailer should ask the customer to retain the affected product, original packaging and any remaining contents while the matter is investigated. The Batch Number and Best Before Date should be recorded as early as reasonably practicable.</p>
<p>Where the Retailer possesses potentially affected stock, relevant products should be handled in accordance with Section 19. Apex Feeds may request physical return of affected product or packaging where reasonably necessary for investigation.</p>
<h4 id="s25-4">25.4 Precautionary Quarantine</h4>
<p>Where a complaint provides reasonable grounds to suspect that other stock held by the Retailer may be affected, the Retailer should temporarily quarantine the relevant product or Batch Number pending further guidance from Apex Feeds. A single unverified complaint does not automatically require every Apex Feeds product to be removed from sale. The scope of any precautionary quarantine should be proportionate to the nature and credibility of the concern and the products or batches potentially involved.</p>
<h4 id="s25-5">25.5 No Assumption of Causation</h4>
<p>The fact that an animal becomes ill, is injured or dies after consuming or being exposed to an Apex Feeds product does not, by itself, establish that the product caused or contributed to that outcome. Animal health outcomes may be influenced by numerous factors, including underlying health conditions, husbandry, environmental conditions, other foods or supplements, feeding practices and circumstances unrelated to the Apex Feeds product.</p>
<p>The Retailer should avoid representing to the customer or publicly stating that Apex Feeds caused an adverse event unless that conclusion has been reasonably established. Likewise, the Retailer should not dismiss a genuine safety complaint merely because another cause may be possible.</p>
<h4 id="s25-6">25.6 Veterinary Matters</h4>
<p>Retailers must not represent themselves as providing veterinary diagnosis on behalf of Apex Feeds unless independently qualified and legally authorised to do so. Where an animal is seriously unwell or injured, the customer should be encouraged to seek appropriate veterinary advice. Apex Feeds may reasonably request veterinary information relevant to an investigation where the customer is willing and legally able to provide it.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds does not require Retailers to obtain confidential veterinary or customer information beyond what is reasonably necessary and lawfully available for investigation of the complaint.</p>
<h4 id="s25-7">25.7 Customer Communications</h4>
<p>Retailers should handle product and animal-safety complaints professionally and avoid speculation while an investigation is underway. Retailers may accurately tell customers that the matter has been referred to Apex Feeds for investigation. Nothing in this Agreement requires a Retailer to conceal a genuine complaint, prevent a customer from expressing their experience or make a statement the Retailer believes to be false.</p>
<h4 id="s25-8">25.8 Social Media and Public Complaints</h4>
<p>Where an animal-safety complaint is made publicly or through social media, the Retailer should avoid publicly debating technical causation before sufficient information is available. Where appropriate, the Retailer may acknowledge the concern, obtain relevant product information and move detailed investigation into an appropriate direct communication channel. This does not prevent the Retailer from correcting clearly inaccurate factual information or providing established Apex Feeds product information.</p>
<h4 id="s25-9">25.9 Multiple Similar Complaints</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds or a Retailer becomes aware of multiple similar safety complaints potentially associated with the same product, Batch Number or production period, the matter should be escalated promptly.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds may request information from other Authorised Retailers, identify remaining stock, place affected stock into precautionary quarantine or initiate the traceability procedures under Section 26. Where appropriate, Apex Feeds may initiate a product withdrawal or recall under Section 27.</p>
<h4 id="s25-10">25.10 Retailer Cooperation</h4>
<p>Retailers must reasonably cooperate with an Apex Feeds investigation into a genuine product or animal-safety concern. This may include providing relevant stock quantities, Batch Numbers, Best Before Dates, photographs, customer complaint information lawfully available to the Retailer and information concerning remaining affected stock. Requests must be reasonably related and proportionate to the safety investigation.</p>
<h4 id="s25-11">25.11 Customer Privacy</h4>
<p>Retailers should not provide Apex Feeds with unnecessary customer personal information. Where customer contact details, veterinary information or other personal information may materially assist an investigation, the Retailer should handle and disclose that information in accordance with applicable privacy requirements and any necessary customer consent.</p>
<p>Where practical, information may be provided in de-identified form where customer identity is unnecessary to investigate the issue.</p>
<h4 id="s25-12">25.12 Product Testing or Examination</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may arrange examination, testing or other reasonable assessment of affected product where appropriate to the nature of the complaint. The scope of any testing will depend upon the alleged issue, available product, Batch information and circumstances of the complaint. The decision to conduct testing, or the absence of testing where it would not reasonably assist the investigation, does not itself establish whether the product was defective.</p>
<h4 id="s25-13">25.13 Investigation Outcome</h4>
<p>Following reasonable investigation, Apex Feeds may determine that:</p>
<ul>
<li>the available evidence supports a product-related issue;</li>
<li>additional investigation is required;</li>
<li>the issue appears isolated to an individual product;</li>
<li>a broader Batch-related concern may exist;</li>
<li>available evidence indicates another likely cause;</li>
<li>insufficient evidence remains to reliably determine the cause; or</li>
<li>the product appears to conform to its applicable manufacturing and quality requirements.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where reasonably appropriate, Apex Feeds will communicate the relevant outcome or next steps to the Retailer.</p>
<h4 id="s25-14">25.14 Customer Remedies and Wholesale Claims</h4>
<p>Any customer refund, replacement or other consumer remedy remains subject to Section 20 and applicable law. Any corresponding claim by the Retailer against Apex Feeds will be assessed under Sections 21–24. The existence of an animal-safety complaint does not automatically establish entitlement to compensation, nor does the absence of established causation prevent a remedy where applicable law otherwise requires one.</p>
<h4 id="s25-15">25.15 Serious or Broader Safety Risk</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds reasonably believes that a product may present a serious or broader safety risk, Apex Feeds may direct Retailers to immediately cease sale, quarantine affected stock and take other reasonable interim protective measures.</p>
<p>Such action may be taken before the cause of the issue has been conclusively established where precautionary action is reasonably warranted. A precautionary stop-sale or quarantine does not constitute an admission that the product is defective or that Apex Feeds is legally responsible for any reported event.</p>
<h4 id="s25-16">25.16 Regulatory Cooperation</h4>
<p>Where a product-safety issue gives rise to a legal or regulatory reporting, investigation, withdrawal or recall requirement, Apex Feeds and the Retailer must reasonably cooperate with applicable lawful requirements.</p>
<p>Nothing in this Agreement prevents either party from making a report to, communicating with or complying with a lawful direction from an applicable government, regulatory, biosecurity or animal-welfare authority.</p>
<h4 id="s25-17">25.17 Non-Excludable Rights</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section excludes, restricts or modifies any right, remedy, obligation or liability that cannot lawfully be excluded, restricted or modified.</p>
<h2 id="part-f">Part F: Quality Control</h2>
<h3 id="s26">26. Batch Traceability</h3>
<p>Apex Feeds maintains product identification and Batch traceability systems to support quality control, investigation of product concerns and, where necessary, targeted product withdrawal or recall.</p>
<p>Authorised Retailers form an important part of this traceability system and must preserve the identifying information supplied with Apex Feeds products and reasonably cooperate with Batch-related enquiries. The purpose of these requirements is to allow affected products to be identified efficiently without imposing unnecessary customer-recording or administrative requirements upon Retailers during ordinary trade.</p>
<h4 id="s26-1">26.1 Batch Number and Best Before Date</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds products will carry a Batch Number (BN) and Best Before Date (BBD) on the product label or another location on the product or packaging where the information can be reasonably identified. These identifiers assist Apex Feeds in connecting products with relevant production, manufacturing and quality-control information. Retailers must not intentionally remove, obscure, alter, replace or falsify Batch Number or Best Before Date information.</p>
<h4 id="s26-2">26.2 Apex Feeds Batch Records</h4>
<p> Apex Feeds will maintain reasonable production and supply records sufficient to support traceability of manufactured products.</p>
<p>Depending upon the product and manufacturing process, these records may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>product and formulation identification;</li>
<li>Batch Number;</li>
<li>production or packaging date;</li>
<li>Best Before Date;</li>
<li>relevant ingredient or production information;</li>
<li>quantity produced;</li>
<li>quality-control information where applicable;</li>
<li>wholesale orders or Retailers supplied with the Batch; and</li>
<li>other information reasonably necessary for product traceability.</li>
</ul>
<p>The level of information maintained may vary according to the nature of the product and applicable legal or operational requirements.</p>
<h4 id="s26-3">26.3 Retailer Identification of Stock</h4>
<p>Retailers must maintain Apex Feeds products in a manner that allows the applicable Batch Number and Best Before Date to remain reasonably identifiable while the product remains in their possession.</p>
<p>Retailers are not required to create a separate Batch-tracking register for every ordinary retail sale unless required by law, an applicable product-specific requirement or an expressly agreed traceability program.</p>
<h4 id="s26-4">26.4 Ordinary Retail Sales</h4>
<p>Unless otherwise required by law, Retailers are not required to record the identity of every customer who purchases an Apex Feeds product during ordinary retail trade. The absence of individual customer details does not constitute a failure of traceability under this Agreement.</p>
<p>Retailers should, however, maintain their ordinary sales and inventory records in accordance with their normal business practices and applicable law.</p>
<h4 id="s26-5">26.5 Batch Enquiries</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds has reasonable grounds to investigate a particular product or Batch Number, it may contact Retailers reasonably believed to have received or held affected stock.</p>
<p>The Retailer must reasonably assist Apex Feeds in determining, where available:</p>
<ul>
<li>whether affected stock remains on hand;</li>
<li>approximate quantity remaining;</li>
<li>location of affected stock;</li>
<li>quantity originally received;</li>
<li>approximate quantity already sold or otherwise used;</li>
<li>whether relevant customer complaints have been received; and</li>
<li>other information reasonably necessary to establish the distribution and remaining location of the affected Batch.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apex Feeds will not require unrelated customer, sales or business information merely because a Batch enquiry has been initiated.</p>
<h4 id="s26-6">26.6 Multiple Retail Locations</h4>
<p>Where an Authorised Retailer operates multiple locations under the same approved legal entity and ABN, the Retailer should be reasonably capable of identifying which locations received or currently hold affected stock where that information is available through its ordinary inventory or transfer records.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds may request that affected stock be identified across all locations operated under the approved wholesale account.</p>
<h4 id="s26-7">26.7 Internal Stock Transfers</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds products are transferred between approved locations operated by the same legal entity, the Batch Number and Best Before Date must remain identifiable. Retailers should use reasonable internal inventory practices sufficient to assist in locating affected stock if a Batch investigation subsequently occurs.</p>
<p>This requirement does not require a particular inventory-management system or software platform.</p>
<h4 id="s26-8">26.8 Retailer-Repacked Live Feeders</h4>
<p>Where bulk live feeder insects are divided into smaller Retailer-packed quantities in accordance with Section 17, the Retailer is not required to represent those smaller containers as individually packed by Apex Feeds.</p>
<p>However, the Retailer should maintain reasonable records sufficient to identify the Apex Feeds bulk supply from which Retailer-packed live feeders originated where reasonably practicable. The precise method of recording this information may be determined by the Retailer and does not require Apex Feeds-branded retail labels.</p>
<h4 id="s26-9">26.9 Product Identification Must Remain Intact</h4>
<p>Retailers must not intentionally remove or obscure product information necessary to identify genuine Apex Feeds manufactured stock. Price stickers, inventory labels, security labels or other Retailer markings may be added provided they do not materially obscure the Batch Number, Best Before Date, product identity, required warnings or other important product information.</p>
<h4 id="s26-10">26.10 Missing or Unreadable Batch Information</h4>
<p>Where a Batch Number or Best Before Date becomes detached, damaged or unreadable, the Retailer must not estimate, invent or independently recreate the missing information. Where the correct information can be reliably established, Apex Feeds may provide replacement identification or other appropriate instructions.</p>
<p>Where the product cannot be reliably identified and traceability is reasonably necessary for safe sale, the product should be removed from sale pending guidance from Apex Feeds.</p>
<h4 id="s26-11">26.11 Customer Complaints and Batch Information</h4>
<p>When investigating a potential manufacturing, quality or animal-safety complaint, Retailers should obtain and record the Batch Number and Best Before Date wherever reasonably possible. A missing Batch Number does not automatically invalidate a genuine complaint, but may affect Apex Feeds’ ability to identify the production Batch and investigate the alleged issue.</p>
<h4 id="s26-12">26.12 Precautionary Batch Hold</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds identifies reasonable grounds for concern regarding a particular Batch, it may direct affected Retailers to temporarily cease sale and quarantine remaining stock while the matter is investigated. A precautionary Batch hold may be initiated before a defect has been conclusively established where doing so is reasonably appropriate to protect product integrity, animal safety or traceability. A precautionary hold does not, by itself, constitute an admission that the Batch is defective.</p>
<h4 id="s26-13">26.13 Scope of Batch Hold</h4>
<p>Where practicable, a Batch hold should be limited to the specific product, Batch Number, production period or other identifiable stock reasonably connected to the concern. Apex Feeds will not require unrelated products or Batches to be removed from sale without reasonable grounds. Where investigation indicates that the potential issue may extend beyond the initially identified Batch, the scope of the hold may be reasonably expanded.</p>
<h4 id="s26-14">26.14 Stock Under Batch Hold</h4>
<p>Stock subject to a Batch hold must:</p>
<ul>
<li>be removed from retail sale;</li>
<li>be clearly separated or otherwise identified as not for sale;</li>
<li>remain under appropriate storage conditions;</li>
<li>not be transferred, repacked, used, fed, sold or disposed of unless instructed or reasonably necessary for immediate safety or legal reasons; and</li>
<li>remain reasonably identifiable by product, Batch Number and quantity.</li>
</ul>
<p>The Retailer does not need a dedicated quarantine room or specialised quarantine equipment where affected stock can otherwise be securely and appropriately isolated.</p>
<h4 id="s26-15">26.15 Investigation of a Batch</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may review relevant manufacturing, ingredient, packaging, quality-control, complaint and distribution information when investigating a Batch. Apex Feeds may also request samples or physical return of affected stock where reasonably necessary. Where appropriate, Apex Feeds may compare affected stock with retained samples, other products from the Batch or related production information.</p>
<h4 id="s26-16">26.16 Release of a Batch Hold</h4>
<p>Where investigation reasonably establishes that stock subject to a precautionary hold remains suitable for sale, Apex Feeds may release the affected stock from quarantine. The Retailer may then return suitable stock to normal sale. The fact that stock was temporarily quarantined does not require it to be discarded, discounted or treated as defective where the concern has been satisfactorily resolved.</p>
<h4 id="s26-17">26.17 Escalation of a Batch Concern</h4>
<p>Where investigation identifies a confirmed or reasonably suspected issue requiring broader action, Apex Feeds may escalate the matter to a product withdrawal or recall under Section 27. Retailers must then comply with the applicable withdrawal or recall instructions.</p>
<h4 id="s26-18">26.18 Confidentiality During Investigation</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may request that non-public manufacturing, investigation or Batch information supplied to a Retailer during an active investigation be treated confidentially where reasonably necessary.</p>
<p>This does not prevent a Retailer from:</p>
<ul>
<li>complying with law or a regulatory authority;</li>
<li>providing necessary information to affected customers;</li>
<li>obtaining professional advice;</li>
<li>communicating with its insurer; or</li>
<li>taking reasonable steps necessary to protect animal or public safety.</li>
</ul>
<p>Nothing in this clause requires a Retailer to conceal a known safety risk.</p>
<h4 id="s26-19">26.19 Records and Cooperation</h4>
<p>Retailers are expected to provide reasonable cooperation with Batch traceability enquiries using information available through their ordinary business records. Apex Feeds does not require Retailers to maintain unnecessarily detailed records solely for Apex Feeds’ benefit unless a particular record is reasonably necessary for product safety, traceability, regulatory compliance or an expressly agreed program.</p>
<h4 id="s26-20">26.20 Non-Excludable Legal and Regulatory Requirements</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section limits any record-keeping, traceability, reporting, withdrawal or recall obligation imposed by applicable law or a lawful regulatory authority. Where a legal requirement imposes a higher traceability standard than this Agreement, the applicable legal requirement prevails.</p>
<h3 id="s27">27. Product Withdrawal &amp; Recall</h3>
<p>Apex Feeds maintains procedures for the precautionary holding, withdrawal and, where necessary, recall of products where a quality, safety, manufacturing, labelling, regulatory or other material concern is identified.</p>
<p>Authorised Retailers must reasonably cooperate with product withdrawal and recall instructions issued by Apex Feeds and with any applicable lawful direction issued by a government or regulatory authority. Actions taken under this Section should be proportionate to the nature and extent of the identified or reasonably suspected issue.</p>
<h4 id="s27-1">27.1 Levels of Product Action</h4>
<p>Depending upon the circumstances, Apex Feeds may initiate:</p>
<p>(a) Precautionary Batch Hold A temporary stop-sale and quarantine while an issue is investigated, as described in Section 26. A Batch hold does not establish that the product is defective and may subsequently be lifted.</p>
<p>(b) Product Withdrawal Removal of identified product from sale or distribution where Apex Feeds determines that the product should no longer remain in ordinary trade, but the circumstances do not necessarily require a broader customer-level recall.</p>
<p>(c) Product Recall A coordinated action to remove or recover affected product from the supply chain and, where appropriate, from customers or end users because Apex Feeds or an applicable authority determines that recall action is reasonably necessary. The use of a precautionary hold or withdrawal does not prevent Apex Feeds from subsequently escalating the matter to a recall if further information warrants that action.</p>
<h4 id="s27-2">27.2 Grounds for Withdrawal or Recall</h4>
<p>A product withdrawal or recall may be initiated where Apex Feeds reasonably identifies or suspects a material issue involving:</p>
<ul>
<li>product or animal safety;</li>
<li>contamination;</li>
<li>foreign material;</li>
<li>incorrect formulation or ingredients;</li>
<li>significant manufacturing failure;</li>
<li>material packaging failure;</li>
<li>materially incorrect or missing product information;</li>
<li>Batch identification or traceability failure;</li>
<li>regulatory or legal non-compliance;</li>
<li>repeated or significant quality complaints;</li>
<li>an issue affecting multiple products or Batches;</li>
<li>a direction or requirement from an applicable authority; or</li>
<li>another circumstance reasonably requiring affected product to be removed from sale or distribution.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apex Feeds does not need to wait until every aspect of an issue has been conclusively established where precautionary action is reasonably necessary to protect animal safety, customers, Retailers or product integrity.</p>
<h4 id="s27-3">27.3 Apex Feeds Withdrawal or Recall Notice</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds initiates a withdrawal or recall, it will provide affected Retailers with information reasonably necessary to identify and manage the affected stock.</p>
<p>Where available and applicable, the notice may identify:</p>
<ul>
<li>affected product;</li>
<li>product variety or size;</li>
<li>Batch Number or Batch Numbers;</li>
<li>Best Before Date;</li>
<li>reason or general nature of the action;</li>
<li>whether the action is a withdrawal or recall;</li>
<li>action required from the Retailer;</li>
<li>whether customer communication is required;</li>
<li>instructions concerning return, holding or disposal of affected stock;</li>
<li>information that may be provided to customers; and * relevant contact details or further instructions. Instructions may be updated as additional information becomes available.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s27-4">27.4 Immediate Retailer Action</h4>
<p>Upon receiving a withdrawal or recall instruction applicable to stock held by the Retailer, the Retailer must act promptly and:</p>
<ul>
<li>cease sale and supply of affected stock;</li>
<li>remove affected stock from physical retail displays;</li>
<li>remove or disable affected stock from online sale;</li>
<li>quarantine remaining affected stock;</li>
<li>prevent affected stock from being transferred between locations except as specifically instructed;</li>
<li>identify the quantity of affected stock remaining;</li>
<li>preserve Batch Number and Best Before Date information; and</li>
<li>follow reasonable return, disposal or other instructions issued by Apex Feeds.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where multiple locations operate under the same approved legal entity, the Retailer must reasonably communicate the action to all affected locations.</p>
<h4 id="s27-5">27.5 Identification of Affected Stock</h4>
<p>Retailers should use the product, Batch Number, Best Before Date and other identifying information supplied by Apex Feeds to determine which stock is affected. Where a Retailer is uncertain whether particular stock falls within the withdrawal or recall, the stock should remain quarantined until its status can reasonably be confirmed. Stock clearly outside the identified scope does not need to be removed from sale unless Apex Feeds or an applicable authority subsequently expands the action.</p>
<h4 id="s27-6">27.6 Online and Promotional Material</h4>
<p>Where affected product is subject to withdrawal or recall, Retailers must promptly stop actively offering the affected stock for sale through their authorised online and social channels. Retailers are not required to remove historical social media posts merely because they previously advertised the product, unless removal or correction is reasonably necessary to prevent customers from being misled about current availability or safety.</p>
<h4 id="s27-7">27.7 Customer-Level Recall</h4>
<p>Where a recall requires action beyond stock remaining within the Retailer’s possession, Apex Feeds will determine, having regard to applicable law and regulatory requirements, the reasonable customer-communication steps required. Retailers must reasonably cooperate with those steps.</p>
<p>This may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>displaying a recall notice;</li>
<li>publishing information through the Retailer’s website or social media;</li>
<li>contacting identifiable affected customers where reasonably practicable;</li>
<li>providing customers with Apex Feeds-approved recall information;</li>
<li>accepting returned affected products; or</li>
<li>directing customers to an appropriate return or contact process.</li>
</ul>
<p>Retailers are not ordinarily required to maintain customer-identification records for every Apex Feeds retail sale merely to facilitate a possible future recall.</p>
<h4 id="s27-8">27.8 Customer Communications</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may provide Retailers with approved wording, notices, graphics, frequently asked questions or other information for use during a withdrawal or recall. Retailers should communicate the relevant facts accurately and avoid unnecessary speculation concerning the cause, extent or consequences of an issue while an investigation remains underway.</p>
<p>Retailers may answer ordinary customer questions and provide their own reasonable customer-service assistance, provided they do not materially misrepresent the withdrawal or recall.</p>
<h4 id="s27-9">27.9 No Concealment of Safety Information</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Agreement requires a Retailer to conceal a genuine product-safety concern, provide information it believes to be false or withhold information that must lawfully be provided to a customer or authority. Where Apex Feeds requests consistency in recall communications, that requirement is intended to ensure accurate and coordinated information rather than prevent lawful disclosure of safety information.</p>
<h4 id="s27-10">27.10 Returned Customer Stock</h4>
<p>Where customers return products affected by a recall, the Retailer must keep returned affected stock separate from normal saleable inventory. Returned recalled product must not be resold, repurposed, used as samples, fed to animals or otherwise redistributed unless Apex Feeds expressly confirms that the product is suitable and authorises that action. The Retailer should preserve identifying information where reasonably possible.</p>
<h4 id="s27-11">27.11 Return, Collection or Disposal</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may instruct Retailers to:</p>
<ul>
<li>retain affected stock pending collection;</li>
<li>return affected stock to Apex Feeds;</li>
<li>make stock available to an authorised carrier;</li>
<li>safely dispose of affected stock;</li>
<li>provide reasonable evidence of disposal; or</li>
<li>take another reasonable action appropriate to the circumstances.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where Apex Feeds requires return or collection of stock subject to an Apex-initiated withdrawal or recall, Apex Feeds will ordinarily arrange or bear the reasonable associated return freight unless applicable law or the circumstances require otherwise.</p>
<h4 id="s27-12">27.12 Disposal Evidence</h4>
<p>Where affected stock is authorised or directed to be destroyed or disposed of, Apex Feeds may reasonably request evidence sufficient to confirm that the identified stock has been removed from circulation. Depending upon the circumstances, this may include photographs, quantity records, Batch information or another reasonable form of confirmation. Apex Feeds will not require unnecessarily burdensome destruction procedures where simpler evidence reasonably establishes disposal.</p>
<h4 id="s27-13">27.13 Retailer Stock Remedy</h4>
<p>Where saleable stock purchased directly from Apex Feeds is removed from sale as part of an Apex Feeds-initiated withdrawal or recall and cannot subsequently be returned to sale, Apex Feeds will provide an appropriate wholesale remedy in accordance with Section 21 and applicable law.</p>
<p>The remedy may include replacement stock, wholesale account credit, refund of the applicable wholesale purchase value or another appropriate arrangement. Unless otherwise required by law or agreed by Apex Feeds, the product-value component of the remedy is based upon the actual wholesale value paid or payable by the Retailer for the affected stock, rather than the recommended or actual retail selling price.</p>
<h4 id="s27-14">27.14 Customer Refunds</h4>
<p>During a Recall Where affected product has already been sold to customers, Retailers must provide any customer remedy required by applicable law or the applicable recall instructions. Where Apex Feeds establishes a specific customer-return arrangement for a recall, Retailers should follow that process where reasonably practicable. The financial treatment between Apex Feeds and the Retailer will be determined in accordance with the recall arrangements, Section 21 and applicable law.</p>
<h4 id="s27-15">27.15 Retailer Expenses</h4>
<p>Where a withdrawal or recall is attributable to an Apex Feeds issue, Apex Feeds will reasonably consider necessary and directly incurred Retailer costs arising from compliance with specific Apex Feeds recall instructions. Ordinary staff time, anticipated profit, general business overhead, reputational loss or other indirect commercial loss is not automatically reimbursable merely because a withdrawal or recall occurred, subject to applicable law. Where Apex Feeds requires unusual or material work from a Retailer beyond ordinary removal and handling of affected stock, the parties should discuss the reasonable arrangements for that work.</p>
<h4 id="s27-16">27.16 Stock Already Sold</h4>
<p>Where affected stock has already been sold and individual customers cannot reasonably be identified, the Retailer will not be treated as non-compliant merely because customer identity was not recorded during ordinary retail trade. The Retailer must nevertheless cooperate with reasonable broader notification measures determined by Apex Feeds or required by an applicable authority.</p>
<h4 id="s27-17">27.17 Retailer-Initiated Safety Action</h4>
<p>Nothing prevents a Retailer from temporarily removing or quarantining stock where it reasonably believes an immediate product or animal-safety concern may exist. The Retailer should notify Apex Feeds promptly and provide available information so the concern can be investigated. A Retailer should not publicly describe a precautionary store-level hold as an official Apex Feeds recall unless Apex Feeds or an applicable authority has actually initiated or confirmed a recall.</p>
<h4 id="s27-18">27.18 Regulatory Authorities</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may notify, consult or cooperate with applicable government, regulatory, biosecurity, animal-welfare or other competent authorities where required or reasonably appropriate. Retailers must comply with lawful directions applying to them and reasonably assist Apex Feeds with information required for regulatory investigation, withdrawal or recall activity. Nothing in this Agreement prevents a Retailer from communicating directly with an applicable authority or complying with its own legal reporting obligations.</p>
<h4 id="s27-19">27.19 Expansion or Reduction of Recall Scope</h4>
<p>As an investigation develops, Apex Feeds may reasonably expand or reduce the products, Batch Numbers, Best Before Dates, production periods or other stock included within a withdrawal or recall. Retailers must implement updated instructions promptly. Where stock is formally released from a precautionary action and remains suitable for sale, it may be returned to normal inventory.</p>
<h4 id="s27-20">27.20 Recall Records</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may maintain records relating to a withdrawal or recall, including:</p>
<ul>
<li>affected products and Batches;</li>
<li>production and distribution information;</li>
<li>Retailers supplied;</li>
<li>quantities identified, quarantined, returned or disposed of;</li>
<li>customer complaints where relevant;</li>
<li>communications issued;</li>
<li>corrective actions;</li>
<li>investigation findings; and</li>
<li>other information reasonably necessary for quality-control, regulatory or legal purposes.</li>
</ul>
<p>Retailers must maintain or provide reasonable records relating to affected stock where required by law or reasonably necessary to complete the withdrawal or recall.</p>
<h4 id="s27-21">27.21 Failure to Comply With a Recall</h4>
<p>A Retailer’s deliberate or serious failure to comply with a lawful and reasonable product recall instruction may constitute a serious breach of this Agreement. This may include knowingly continuing to sell, transfer or distribute stock confirmed as subject to recall after the Retailer has received clear notice of the recall. An inadvertent administrative error, reasonable misunderstanding or delay outside the Retailer’s control will not automatically constitute serious breach where the Retailer acts promptly to correct the issue once identified.</p>
<h4 id="s27-22">27.22 Completion of Withdrawal or Recall</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds will determine when an Apex Feeds-initiated withdrawal or recall has been completed or when affected Retailers may cease the applicable special procedures, subject to any requirements or directions of an applicable authority. Apex Feeds may subsequently review the incident and implement reasonable corrective or preventative measures concerning manufacturing, packaging, labelling, storage, distribution or product information.</p>
<h4 id="s27-23">27.23 No Admission of Liability</h4>
<p>A precautionary hold, withdrawal, recall, customer notification, replacement, refund or other protective action does not, by itself, constitute an admission of negligence, defect, causation or legal liability by Apex Feeds. Protective action may be taken where reasonably warranted before the precise cause of an issue has been conclusively established.</p>
<h4 id="s27-24">27.24 Non-Excludable Rights and Regulatory Requirements</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section excludes, restricts or modifies any right, remedy, obligation, reporting requirement or liability that cannot lawfully be excluded, restricted or modified.</p>
<p>Where applicable legislation or a lawful regulatory direction requires a different or additional recall procedure, that requirement prevails to the extent of any inconsistency with this Agreement.</p>
<h2 id="part-g">Part G: Agreement Management</h2>
<h3 id="s28">28. Confidentiality</h3>
<p>During the wholesale relationship, Apex Feeds and an Authorised Retailer may exchange commercial, operational, financial or other information that is not publicly available and which a reasonable business would understand to be confidential.</p>
<p>Each party must take reasonable steps to protect confidential information received from the other and must not use or disclose that information except for the purposes of the wholesale relationship, as authorised by the party that supplied it, or as otherwise permitted under this Section.</p>
<h4 id="s28-1">28.1 Apex Feeds Confidential Information</h4>
<p>Confidential information of Apex Feeds may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>non-public wholesale pricing and price lists;</li>
<li>account-specific discounts, rebates, promotions or commercial arrangements;</li>
<li>credit limits and payment arrangements;</li>
<li>confidential quotations;</li>
<li>non-public product development information;</li>
<li>products, formulations or ranges not yet publicly released;</li>
<li>non-public manufacturing or production information;</li>
<li>non-public supplier or sourcing information;</li>
<li>internal quality-control information;</li>
<li>confidential Batch investigation information;</li>
<li>non-public withdrawal or recall investigations;</li>
<li>business plans, commercial strategies or future product plans;</li>
<li>non-public sales, distribution or retailer information;</li>
<li>passwords, wholesale portal credentials or protected wholesale resources; and</li>
<li>other information that Apex Feeds identifies as confidential or which a reasonable Retailer would understand from the circumstances to be commercially confidential.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s28-2">28.2 Retailer Confidential Information</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds must similarly protect confidential information supplied by the Retailer.</p>
<p>This may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>account-specific financial or credit information;</li>
<li>non-public purchasing information;</li>
<li>confidential business plans;</li>
<li>non-public sales or inventory information supplied during an investigation;</li>
<li>confidential customer information;</li>
<li>passwords or account-security information; and</li>
<li>other non-public information which a reasonable business would understand to be confidential. Nothing in this Agreement gives Apex Feeds unrestricted rights to use the Retailer’s confidential business information for unrelated purposes.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s28-3">28.3 Information That Is Not Confidential</h4>
<p>Information is not confidential merely because it relates to Apex Feeds or the Retailer.</p>
<p>Confidentiality obligations do not ordinarily apply to information that:</p>
<ul>
<li>is already lawfully available to the public;</li>
<li>subsequently becomes public through no breach of this Agreement;</li>
<li>was lawfully known to the receiving party before disclosure;</li>
<li>is independently developed without use of the confidential information;</li>
<li>is lawfully obtained from another source without a confidentiality obligation;</li>
<li>has been expressly authorised for public disclosure; or</li>
<li>is clearly intended for customer, retailer or public distribution.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s28-4">28.4 Public Product Information</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds product information intended for customer or retailer education is not treated as confidential merely because Apex Feeds created it.</p>
<p>This includes applicable:</p>
<ul>
<li>public website product descriptions;</li>
<li>approved long-form product descriptions supplied for retailer use;</li>
<li>product labels;</li>
<li>ingredient information;</li>
<li>nutritional information;</li>
<li>feeding and preparation instructions;</li>
<li>public advertising materials;</li>
<li>retailer brochures or educational materials intended for customer use; and</li>
<li>public social media content. Retailers may use such information in accordance with Sections 10–12.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s28-5">28.5 Wholesale Pricing</h4>
<p>Non-public wholesale pricing is commercially confidential and is supplied for the Retailer’s own purchasing and account-management purposes. Retailers must not intentionally publish or distribute Apex Feeds’ non-public wholesale price lists, account-specific discounts or confidential commercial terms to unrelated third parties.</p>
<p>This does not prevent the Retailer from:</p>
<ul>
<li>determining its own retail selling prices;</li>
<li>discussing relevant information internally with authorised staff;</li>
<li>providing information to its accountant, bookkeeper, lawyer or professional adviser;</li>
<li>providing information to a lender, insurer or other professional service provider where reasonably necessary and subject to appropriate confidentiality; or</li>
<li>making a disclosure required by law. Nothing in this clause restricts the Retailer’s independent retail pricing rights under Section 6.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s28-6">28.6 Employees and Representatives</h4>
<p>Each party may disclose confidential information to employees, managers, contractors, professional advisers or other representatives who reasonably require the information for legitimate purposes connected with the wholesale relationship. The party making the disclosure must take reasonable steps to ensure that the recipient understands the confidential nature of the information where that would not otherwise be apparent.</p>
<h4 id="s28-7">28.7 Product Development and Unreleased Products</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds provides a Retailer with information, samples, photographs, formulations, packaging concepts or other material concerning a product that has not yet been publicly released, the Retailer must not publicly disclose that information before the applicable public release unless Apex Feeds has authorised disclosure.</p>
<p>Where Apex Feeds wishes a particular testing or development program to carry additional confidentiality requirements, those requirements should be communicated to participating Retailers before the confidential material is supplied.</p>
<h4 id="s28-8">28.8 Feedback and Product Testing</h4>
<p>Retailer participation in product testing, trials or feedback programs does not transfer ownership of Apex Feeds’ confidential product-development information to the Retailer. Retailers remain free to provide genuine feedback, criticism, suggestions and observations to Apex Feeds. Any additional confidentiality requirements applying to a particular pre-release testing program should be agreed or clearly communicated before participation.</p>
<h4 id="s28-9">28.9 Quality Investigations</h4>
<p>During a product-quality, Batch, safety, withdrawal or recall investigation, Apex Feeds may provide affected Retailers with non-public information necessary to manage the issue. Retailers must treat genuinely confidential investigation information appropriately.</p>
<p>However, confidentiality does not prevent a Retailer from communicating information reasonably necessary to protect customers or animals, comply with a recall, obtain professional advice or satisfy a legal or regulatory obligation.</p>
<h4 id="s28-10">28.10 Customer Information</h4>
<p>Neither party is required to disclose unnecessary customer personal information merely because a wholesale, quality or safety investigation is underway. Where customer information is reasonably necessary, the parties should limit disclosure to information relevant to the legitimate purpose for which it is required and handle it in accordance with applicable privacy requirements.</p>
<h4 id="s28-11">28.11 Required Disclosure</h4>
<p>A party may disclose confidential information where reasonably necessary to:</p>
<ul>
<li>comply with applicable law;</li>
<li>comply with a court order or lawful regulatory requirement;</li>
<li>communicate with a government or regulatory authority;</li>
<li>obtain legal, accounting, insurance or other professional advice;</li>
<li>enforce or defend its legal rights; or</li>
<li>respond to an immediate and genuine safety concern.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where lawful and reasonably practicable, the disclosing party should limit the disclosure to information necessary for that purpose.</p>
<h4 id="s28-12">28.12 No Use for Unrelated Commercial Advantage</h4>
<p>Neither party may knowingly use the other party’s confidential information for an unrelated commercial purpose in a manner inconsistent with the purpose for which the information was provided. For example, access to another party’s confidential pricing, supplier, customer, manufacturing or commercial information does not authorise the receiving party to exploit or distribute that information for an unrelated competitive purpose.</p>
<h4 id="s28-13">28.13 Account Security</h4>
<p>Wholesale website, portal or account login credentials supplied to a Retailer are confidential to the approved Retailer and its authorised representatives. Retailers must take reasonable steps to prevent unauthorised access and must notify Apex Feeds if they become aware that wholesale account credentials may have been compromised.</p>
<h4 id="s28-14">28.14 Accidental Disclosure</h4>
<p>An accidental disclosure of confidential information does not automatically constitute a serious breach where the party responsible acts reasonably after becoming aware of the disclosure. Where appropriate, the party should take reasonable steps to limit further disclosure, recover or remove the information where practicable and notify the other party where the disclosure may materially affect its interests. Deliberate, repeated or reckless disclosure may be addressed under the breach provisions of this Agreement.</p>
<h4 id="s28-15">28.15 Return or Destruction of Confidential Material</h4>
<p>Following termination of the wholesale relationship, either party may reasonably request the return, deletion or destruction of genuinely confidential material belonging to it where continued retention is unnecessary.</p>
<p>This does not require deletion or destruction of:</p>
<ul>
<li>records required to be retained by law;</li>
<li>ordinary accounting or transaction records;</li>
<li>information reasonably required for an existing dispute, claim or legal purpose;</li>
<li>routine electronic backups not reasonably capable of individual deletion; or</li>
<li>public Apex Feeds materials that the Retailer remains entitled to use in connection with genuine remaining stock under Section 10.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s28-16">28.16 Confidentiality Following Termination</h4>
<p>The obligations in this Section continue after termination of the wholesale relationship for so long as the relevant information remains genuinely confidential. Information that subsequently becomes lawfully public is no longer subject to confidentiality merely because it was confidential when originally supplied.</p>
<h4 id="s28-17">28.17 No Restriction on Lawful Reporting</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section prevents either party from making a lawful complaint, report or disclosure to a regulator, government authority, law-enforcement body, court or other body where that disclosure is protected or required by law.</p>
<p>Nothing in this Agreement requires either party to conceal unlawful conduct or a genuine safety concern.</p>
<h3 id="s29">29. Changes to Products &amp; Standards</h3>
<p>Apex Feeds continually develops and improves its products, manufacturing processes, packaging, product information and operational standards. Accordingly, products supplied by Apex Feeds may be reasonably modified, improved, replaced or discontinued over time without requiring a new Authorised Retailer Agreement for each change.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds will take reasonable steps to communicate material changes where those changes affect information that Retailers reasonably require to safely, accurately or effectively store, handle, represent, advertise or sell the affected product.</p>
<h4 id="s29-1">29.1 Product Development and Improvement</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may reasonably modify or improve its products from time to time.</p>
<p>Changes may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>formulation or ingredient adjustments;</li>
<li>ingredient sourcing or supplier changes;</li>
<li>manufacturing or processing methods;</li>
<li>dehydration, freeze-drying, freezing or other production processes;</li>
<li>ingredient size, cut, texture or presentation;</li>
<li>pack size or product quantity;</li>
<li>packaging materials or packaging format;</li>
<li>labels and label design;</li>
<li>product names or range presentation;</li>
<li>preparation or feeding instructions;</li>
<li>storage instructions;</li>
<li>Batch coding or Best Before Date presentation;</li>
<li>nutritional or product information;</li>
<li>manufacturing equipment or production systems; and</li>
<li>other reasonable product or operational improvements.</li>
</ul>
<p>A change of this kind does not, by itself, constitute a breach of this Agreement or require the Retailer’s consent.</p>
<h4 id="s29-2">29.2 Material Product Changes</h4>
<p>Where a change materially affects information reasonably important to the Retailer or customer, Apex Feeds will update or communicate the relevant information as appropriate.</p>
<p>This may include material changes affecting:</p>
<ul>
<li>product identity;</li>
<li>ingredients or composition;</li>
<li>relevant allergen or safety information;</li>
<li>intended animal or species application;</li>
<li>preparation method;</li>
<li>feeding instructions;</li>
<li>storage requirements;</li>
<li>pack size or quantity;</li>
<li>material warnings or precautions; or</li>
<li>another characteristic that would reasonably affect how the product is represented, handled or used.</li>
</ul>
<p>The method and timing of communication may depend upon the nature and significance of the change.</p>
<h4 id="s29-3">29.3 Minor Product Changes</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds is not required to individually notify every Retailer of minor changes that do not materially affect the product’s safe use, identity or ordinary sale. Examples may include minor artwork changes, formatting changes, packaging design improvements, supplier changes that do not materially alter relevant product characteristics, reasonable natural ingredient variation or minor manufacturing improvements. Updated products may enter normal wholesale supply as existing stock is exhausted.</p>
<h4 id="s29-4">29.4 Ingredient Changes</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may change ingredient sources, suppliers or individual ingredients where reasonably necessary for product development, availability, quality, manufacturing or commercial reasons. Where an ingredient change materially changes the represented composition, safety information, preparation, nutritional information or other information reasonably important to the Retailer or customer, Apex Feeds will update the applicable product information. Retailers should rely upon the information applicable to the product and Batch being sold rather than assuming that every historical Batch is identical.</p>
<h4 id="s29-5">29.5 Product Reformulation</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may reformulate an existing product where reasonably appropriate. Where a reformulation materially changes the nature, intended use or important characteristics of the product, Apex Feeds will take reasonable steps to distinguish or communicate the updated formulation so Retailers can accurately represent the stock being sold. A reformulated product does not automatically require creation of a new product name where the product continues to reasonably fall within the existing product identity.</p>
<h4 id="s29-6">29.6 Packaging and Label Changes</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may update packaging, labels, artwork, pack formats or product presentation over time. During a transition, Retailers may hold legitimate Apex Feeds stock displaying both previous and updated packaging. Older packaging does not make genuine stock defective or unsuitable merely because newer artwork or packaging has subsequently been introduced, provided the older stock remains within its applicable Best Before Date, properly stored and otherwise suitable for sale.</p>
<h4 id="s29-7">29.7 Existing Stock Following a Product Change</h4>
<p>Unless Apex Feeds advises otherwise for safety, legal, regulatory or product-integrity reasons, Retailers may continue selling genuine existing stock after Apex Feeds introduces an updated product, formulation, label or packaging design.</p>
<p>Retailers are not ordinarily required to discard or return otherwise saleable previous-version stock solely because a newer version has entered production. Where important differences exist between versions, Retailers must take reasonable care to provide information applicable to the particular stock being sold.</p>
<h4 id="s29-8">29.8 Product Names and Range Structure</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may introduce, rename, reorganise, replace, combine or discontinue products or product ranges as its range develops. A change to a product or range name does not require amendment of this Agreement. Retailers may continue accurately identifying genuine older stock by the name appearing on that product while it remains lawfully saleable.</p>
<h4 id="s29-9">29.9 Pack Sizes and Product Quantities</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may introduce new pack sizes or alter the quantity of product supplied within a retail format. Where the quantity of an existing product changes, the applicable packaging, product information and wholesale pricing will be updated as appropriate. Retailers are responsible for ensuring that advertising and shelf information accurately identifies the size or quantity of the particular product being offered.</p>
<h4 id="s29-10">29.10 Product Discontinuation</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may discontinue a product, formulation, pack size or range for reasonable commercial, manufacturing, supply, regulatory or product-development reasons. Discontinuation does not require Apex Feeds to repurchase remaining saleable Retailer inventory merely because the product will no longer be manufactured. Retailers may continue selling genuine remaining stock while it remains suitable and within its applicable Best Before Date, unless Apex Feeds advises that sale must cease for safety, regulatory or other legally justified reasons.</p>
<h4 id="s29-11">29.11 Temporary Product Unavailability</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds does not guarantee continuous availability of every product or ingredient. Products may become temporarily unavailable because of production capacity, ingredient availability, seasonal supply, livestock or insect production, equipment, packaging supply, quality-control requirements, freight disruption or other reasonable operational circumstances. Temporary unavailability does not constitute a breach of this Agreement merely because the Retailer ordinarily stocks or advertises the product.</p>
<h4 id="s29-12">29.12 Retailer Product Information</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds issues materially updated product information, Retailers should update their active product listings, feeding information, advertising or customer information within a reasonable period where the previous information has become materially inaccurate. Retailers are not required to immediately remove every historical social media post or archived advertisement merely because product information has subsequently changed. However, information actively used to sell current stock should reasonably reflect the applicable product.</p>
<h4 id="s29-13">29.13 Apex Feeds Standards and Guidelines</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may reasonably update operational standards, product-handling requirements, storage guidance, advertising standards, brand guidelines, claims guidance and other procedures associated with the wholesale program. Updates may be incorporated into this Agreement, its Schedules, retailer resources or other written standards issued by Apex Feeds.</p>
<h4 id="s29-14">29.14 Minor Operational Updates</h4>
<p>Minor administrative, procedural or operational updates that do not materially increase the Retailer’s contractual obligations may take effect when reasonably communicated. These may include updated contact details, claim forms, submission procedures, artwork files, product-information resources or similar administrative changes.</p>
<h4 id="s29-15">29.15 Material Changes to Retailer Obligations</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds proposes a change to this Agreement or its mandatory standards that materially increases or alters a Retailer’s contractual obligations, Apex Feeds will provide at least 30 days’ written notice before the change takes effect, unless a shorter period is reasonably necessary under Section 29.16.</p>
<p>The notice should identify the material change sufficiently for the Retailer to understand the effect upon its wholesale relationship. Where a proposed change fundamentally alters the commercial or contractual relationship, Apex Feeds may require the Retailer to acknowledge or accept updated terms before continuing the affected aspect of the wholesale relationship.</p>
<h4 id="s29-16">29.16 Urgent Safety, Legal or Regulatory Changes</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may implement a change immediately or on less than 30 days’ notice where reasonably necessary because of:</p>
<ul>
<li>animal or product safety;</li>
<li>contamination or quality risk;</li>
<li>product withdrawal or recall;</li>
<li>changes in applicable law;</li>
<li>regulatory or government requirements;</li>
<li>biosecurity requirements;</li>
<li>urgent product-integrity concerns; or</li>
<li>another circumstance where delaying the change would create an unreasonable safety, legal or regulatory risk.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apex Feeds will communicate the change as soon as reasonably practicable in the circumstances.</p>
<h4 id="s29-17">29.17 Method of Notification</h4>
<p>Material changes may be communicated through the Retailer’s nominated wholesale email address, wholesale account or portal, formal written notice or another established business communication method reasonably likely to bring the change to the Retailer’s attention. Retailers are responsible for maintaining current contact information in accordance with this Agreement.</p>
<h4 id="s29-18">29.18 Retailer Questions and Transition Support</h4>
<p>Where a material product or standards change requires Retailers to alter product information, handling, storage, advertising or other established practices, Apex Feeds will provide reasonable information necessary to explain the change.</p>
<p>Retailers may contact Apex Feeds where clarification is required. Where reasonably practicable, Apex Feeds may provide updated product descriptions, artwork, retailer information or other materials to assist with implementation.</p>
<h4 id="s29-19">29.19 No Retrospective Breach</h4>
<p>Unless required by law or necessary to address an immediate safety issue, a newly introduced standard will not ordinarily be used to treat conduct occurring before that standard took effect as a breach of this Agreement. Retailers will be expected to comply with the updated standard from its applicable commencement date.</p>
<h4 id="s29-20">29.20 Existing Accepted Orders</h4>
<p>A product, pricing or operational change will not ordinarily alter an order already accepted by Apex Feeds unless:</p>
<ul>
<li>the Retailer agrees;</li>
<li>fulfilment as originally accepted becomes impossible or unlawful;</li>
<li>a safety, quality or regulatory issue prevents supply; or</li>
<li>another provision of this Agreement permits the change.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where Apex Feeds cannot reasonably fulfil an accepted order because of such circumstances, it will communicate with the Retailer and provide an appropriate adjustment, substitution where agreed, credit or refund for the affected supply.</p>
<h4 id="s29-21">29.21 No Guarantee of Identical Future Supply</h4>
<p>Approval of a wholesale account or previous purchase of a product does not guarantee that Apex Feeds will continue indefinitely manufacturing that product in an identical formulation, pack size, packaging format, manufacturing method or presentation.</p>
<p>This clause does not permit Apex Feeds to misrepresent the identity, composition or characteristics of products actually supplied.</p>
<h4 id="s29-22">29.22 Applicable Law</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section permits Apex Feeds to make a change inconsistent with applicable law or to avoid any disclosure, labelling, contractual, safety or other obligation that cannot lawfully be excluded.</p>
<h3 id="s30">30. Compliance</h3>
<p>Authorised Retailers must conduct their activities involving Apex Feeds products in accordance with this Agreement, its applicable Schedules and applicable laws and regulatory requirements. Apex Feeds and the Retailer are each responsible for the legal and regulatory obligations applying to their respective activities.</p>
<p>The purpose of this Section is to support reasonable compliance with the wholesale program and protect product integrity, animal safety, traceability and the reputation of both Apex Feeds and its Authorised Retailers.</p>
<h4 id="s30-1">30.1 Compliance With This Agreement</h4>
<p>The Retailer must reasonably comply with the requirements of this Agreement that apply to its purchase, storage, handling, advertising, representation, sale and supply of Apex Feeds products.</p>
<p>This includes applicable requirements concerning:</p>
<ul>
<li>wholesale account use;</li>
<li>authorised sales channels;</li>
<li>product storage and handling;</li>
<li>frozen products; * live feeders;</li>
<li>stock rotation and expiry;</li>
<li>product identification and traceability;</li>
<li>customer and wholesale claims;</li>
<li>advertising and product representations;</li>
<li>Apex Feeds branding and intellectual property;</li>
<li>product withdrawals and recalls; and</li>
<li>other applicable requirements established under this Agreement.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s30-2">30.2 Applicable Laws</h4>
<p>Each party must comply with laws and regulatory requirements applicable to its own activities.</p>
<p>Depending upon the products, location and activities involved, relevant requirements may include applicable Commonwealth, state, territory and local requirements concerning:</p>
<ul>
<li>animal feed or animal food;</li>
<li>consumer protection;</li>
<li>product safety;</li>
<li>advertising and representations;</li>
<li>biosecurity;</li>
<li>animal welfare;</li>
<li>live animal or feeder insect possession, movement and sale;</li>
<li>environmental requirements;</li>
<li>business licensing and permits;</li>
<li>privacy;</li>
<li>workplace health and safety;</li>
<li>transport and storage; and</li>
<li>other requirements applicable to the particular business or product.</li>
</ul>
<p>Nothing in this Agreement authorises either party to engage in conduct prohibited by law.</p>
<h4 id="s30-3">30.3 Retailer Licences, Permits and Approvals</h4>
<p>The Retailer is responsible for obtaining and maintaining licences, permits, registrations, approvals or other authorisations legally required for its own business activities and location. Approval as an Apex Feeds Authorised Retailer does not represent or guarantee that the Retailer has satisfied every legal requirement applying to its business.</p>
<h4 id="s30-4">30.4 State and Territory Product Restrictions</h4>
<p>Some products, ingredients, live feeder species or related activities may be subject to different requirements or restrictions between Australian jurisdictions. Retailers are responsible for complying with requirements applying to possession, sale, movement or use within the jurisdictions in which they operate or supply customers.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds may restrict, decline or suspend supply of a particular product to a location where it reasonably believes supply may be unlawful or subject to unresolved regulatory or biosecurity restrictions. Such a product-specific restriction does not necessarily terminate the Retailer’s overall wholesale account.</p>
<h4 id="s30-5">30.5 Online and Interstate Sales</h4>
<p>Where a Retailer supplies customers through its authorised website or other permitted channel, the Retailer is responsible for considering applicable restrictions at the customer’s destination. This is particularly relevant to live feeders and other products that may be subject to state or territory biosecurity, possession or movement requirements.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds may provide guidance concerning known restrictions but does not assume the Retailer’s legal responsibility for its independent interstate retail sales.</p>
<h4 id="s30-6">30.6 Staff and Representatives</h4>
<p>The Retailer must take reasonable steps to ensure that owners, managers, employees and other persons acting on its behalf comply with the requirements of this Agreement relevant to their role. The Retailer remains responsible under this Agreement for conduct undertaken on its behalf within the scope of the wholesale relationship.</p>
<p>An isolated staff mistake will not automatically constitute a serious breach where the Retailer responds appropriately and takes reasonable steps to correct the issue.</p>
<h4 id="s30-7">30.7 Internal Staff Training</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds does not require Retailers to establish a formal training program unless separately required for a particular product or legal reason.</p>
<p>However, Retailers should ensure that staff responsible for Apex Feeds products have reasonable access to information necessary for their role, particularly concerning:</p>
<ul>
<li>product storage;</li>
<li>frozen-product handling;</li>
<li>live feeder husbandry;</li>
<li>preparation instructions;</li>
<li>product claims;</li>
<li>customer complaints;</li>
<li>Batch identification; and</li>
<li>withdrawal or recall procedures.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apex Feeds may provide retailer information and educational resources to assist with this process.</p>
<h4 id="s30-8">30.8 Reasonable Compliance Enquiries</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds has reasonable grounds to believe that a material requirement of this Agreement may not be being followed, it may make reasonable enquiries necessary to clarify the issue.</p>
<p>Depending upon the concern, Apex Feeds may request relevant information such as:</p>
<ul>
<li>photographs of product storage or display;</li>
<li>confirmation of freezer or storage conditions;</li>
<li>Batch or inventory information;</li>
<li>copies or screenshots of relevant advertising;</li>
<li>information concerning an authorised sales channel;</li>
<li>information concerning suspected unauthorised resupply;</li>
<li>evidence relating to a product-quality concern; or</li>
<li>other information reasonably connected to the suspected non-compliance.</li>
</ul>
<p>Any request must be proportionate to the issue being investigated.</p>
<h4 id="s30-9">30.9 No Unrestricted Access to Retailer Business Records</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds does not have a general right under this Agreement to inspect unrelated Retailer financial records, customer databases, supplier information or other confidential business information merely because the Retailer holds a wholesale account. Where records are reasonably necessary to investigate a specific compliance, safety, traceability or diversion concern, Apex Feeds may request only information reasonably relevant to that matter.</p>
<h4 id="s30-10">30.10 Physical Verification</h4>
<p>Where there are reasonable grounds to believe that a material product-storage, safety, handling or integrity issue exists and the concern cannot reasonably be resolved through ordinary information or photographs, Apex Feeds may request an agreed inspection of the relevant area of the Retailer’s premises.</p>
<p>Any inspection must:</p>
<ul>
<li>occur at a mutually reasonable time where practicable;</li>
<li>be limited to areas reasonably relevant to the concern;</li>
<li>minimise unreasonable disruption to the Retailer’s business; and</li>
<li>respect unrelated confidential business information.</li>
</ul>
<p>This does not create a general or unrestricted right for Apex Feeds to enter Retailer premises. Urgent circumstances involving a genuine safety or recall issue may require faster cooperation, but do not authorise unlawful entry.</p>
<h4 id="s30-11">30.11 Compliance Concerns</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds identifies an apparent compliance issue, it should communicate the concern to the Retailer with sufficient information for the Retailer to understand what requires attention.</p>
<p>Where reasonably appropriate, the Retailer should be given an opportunity to:</p>
<ul>
<li>explain the circumstances;</li>
<li>provide relevant information;</li>
<li>correct inaccurate information;</li>
<li>remedy the issue;</li>
<li>implement reasonable corrective action; or</li>
<li>otherwise demonstrate compliance.</li>
</ul>
<p>Formal breach and corrective-action procedures are addressed in Section 31.</p>
<h4 id="s30-12">30.12 Immediate Safety Action</h4>
<p>Nothing in Section 30.11 requires Apex Feeds to leave affected product in circulation while providing an ordinary correction period where a genuine and material safety, contamination, biosecurity, recall or legal concern requires immediate action. Apex Feeds may temporarily restrict supply, require quarantine or direct cessation of sale of affected products where reasonably necessary to address such a concern. Any immediate action should, where practicable, be limited to the products, locations or activities reasonably connected to the identified risk.</p>
<h4 id="s30-13">30.13 Regulatory Enquiries</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds becomes aware of a regulatory enquiry concerning Apex Feeds products supplied through a Retailer, the parties must reasonably cooperate to the extent relevant to their respective activities.</p>
<p>Nothing in this Agreement requires either party to:</p>
<ul>
<li>obstruct a regulator;</li>
<li>provide false or misleading information;</li>
<li>conceal a breach of law;</li>
<li>surrender a legal right or privilege; or</li>
<li>refrain from obtaining independent professional advice.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s30-14">30.14 Notification of Material Compliance Issues</h4>
<p>The Retailer should notify Apex Feeds promptly where it becomes aware of a material issue directly affecting its lawful or safe sale of Apex Feeds products.</p>
<p>This may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>suspension or loss of a licence or permit necessary to sell an affected product;</li>
<li>a government direction materially affecting Apex Feeds stock;</li>
<li>a biosecurity restriction affecting live feeders;</li>
<li>a serious storage or equipment failure affecting substantial stock;</li>
<li>a regulatory seizure or stop-sale involving Apex Feeds products; or</li>
<li>another material circumstance reasonably requiring Apex Feeds’ involvement.</li>
</ul>
<p>Retailers are not required to disclose unrelated regulatory or business matters that have no reasonable connection with Apex Feeds or the wholesale relationship.</p>
<h4 id="s30-15">30.15 Apex Feeds Compliance Responsibilities</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds is responsible for its own compliance obligations as manufacturer, supplier or distributor to the extent those obligations apply to its activities. Nothing in this Agreement transfers an Apex Feeds legal responsibility to the Retailer merely because the Retailer resells the product. Similarly, Apex Feeds does not assume a legal responsibility belonging independently to the Retailer merely because the Retailer is an Authorised Retailer.</p>
<h4 id="s30-16">30.16 Good-Faith Compliance Questions</h4>
<p>A Retailer will not be treated as being in breach merely because it asks Apex Feeds for clarification, questions whether a requirement applies, raises a genuine product or compliance concern or reasonably disagrees with Apex Feeds about the interpretation of a requirement. The parties should attempt to resolve genuine uncertainty by reference to the Agreement, applicable product information and, where necessary, applicable legal or regulatory requirements.</p>
<h4 id="s30-17">30.17 Corrective Approach</h4>
<p>Except where immediate action is reasonably necessary, Apex Feeds’ preferred approach to ordinary compliance issues is to identify the concern, communicate it to the Retailer and provide a reasonable opportunity for correction before escalating the matter. Repeated, deliberate, fraudulent, serious or unresolved non-compliance may be addressed under Sections 31–34.</p>
<h4 id="s30-18">30.18 No Waiver Through Previous Non-Enforcement</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds’ failure to immediately identify or act upon a particular instance of non-compliance does not automatically prevent it from requiring future compliance with the Agreement. However, Apex Feeds should communicate a requirement clearly before treating continued conduct as a deliberate or repeated breach where its previous position may reasonably have created uncertainty.</p>
<h4 id="s30-19">30.19 Proportionate Compliance Measures</h4>
<p>Any compliance measure taken by Apex Feeds under this Section should be reasonably proportionate to the nature, seriousness and extent of the concern. Where practicable, action should be directed toward the affected product, activity, sales channel or location rather than unnecessarily disrupting unrelated compliant aspects of the Retailer’s wholesale relationship.</p>
<h4 id="s30-20">30.20 Non-Excludable Rights</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section excludes, restricts or modifies any right, obligation, remedy or liability that cannot lawfully be excluded, restricted or modified.</p>
<h3 id="s31">31. Breach &amp; Corrective Action</h3>
<p>Where an Authorised Retailer fails to comply with a requirement of this Agreement, Apex Feeds may take reasonable and proportionate action having regard to the nature, seriousness, frequency and consequences of the non-compliance.</p>
<p>Except where immediate action is reasonably necessary, Apex Feeds’ preferred approach is to identify the concern, provide the Retailer with a reasonable opportunity to respond and, where appropriate, allow the issue to be corrected before stronger action is taken. Not every breach will justify suspension or termination of the wholesale relationship.</p>
<h4 id="s31-1">31.1 Assessment of a Breach</h4>
<p>When determining the appropriate response to a potential breach, Apex Feeds may reasonably consider:</p>
<ul>
<li>the nature of the requirement involved;</li>
<li>whether the breach was accidental, negligent, reckless or deliberate;</li>
<li>whether the breach is capable of being corrected;</li>
<li>whether the Retailer acted promptly after becoming aware of the issue;</li>
<li>whether customers, animals, product integrity or traceability were placed at risk;</li>
<li>whether the conduct affected Apex Feeds’ brand or intellectual property;</li>
<li>whether the conduct involved dishonesty, diversion or unauthorised resale;</li>
<li>whether similar conduct has occurred previously;</li>
<li>whether Apex Feeds previously provided guidance or a corrective direction;</li>
<li>the Retailer’s explanation and available evidence;</li>
<li>whether the Retailer cooperated with reasonable attempts to resolve the issue; and</li>
<li>the actual or reasonably foreseeable consequences of the conduct.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s31-2">31.2 Minor or Remediable Breach</h4>
<p>A minor or remediable breach is a breach that can reasonably be corrected and does not, in the circumstances, justify immediate suspension or termination.</p>
<p>Examples may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>an outdated product description;</li>
<li>incorrect or outdated promotional information;</li>
<li>a minor branding or artwork issue;</li>
<li>an isolated staff error;</li>
<li>an incorrect product listing;</li>
<li>a minor storage or display issue that has not compromised product;</li>
<li>failure to follow an administrative procedure; * an isolated misunderstanding of a requirement; or</li>
<li>another issue capable of reasonable correction.</li>
</ul>
<p>The existence of one of these circumstances does not automatically establish a breach; the surrounding circumstances remain relevant.</p>
<h4 id="s31-3">31.3 Notice of Breach</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds identifies a remediable breach, it should provide the Retailer with reasonable notice of:</p>
<ul>
<li>the conduct or issue of concern;</li>
<li>the requirement believed to have been breached;</li>
<li>the corrective action reasonably required; and</li>
<li>the period within which the Retailer should respond or correct the issue.</li>
</ul>
<p>Notice may be provided through the Retailer’s nominated wholesale contact details or another established business communication channel.</p>
<h4 id="s31-4">31.4 Opportunity to Respond</h4>
<p>Before materially escalating an ordinary remediable breach, Apex Feeds should give the Retailer a reasonable opportunity to explain the circumstances or provide information showing that:</p>
<ul>
<li>no breach occurred;</li>
<li>the issue has already been corrected;</li>
<li>the Retailer requires clarification;</li>
<li>additional time is reasonably necessary;</li>
<li>circumstances outside the Retailer’s reasonable control affected compliance; or</li>
<li>another reasonable resolution is available.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apex Feeds will consider relevant information provided by the Retailer before determining further action.</p>
<h4 id="s31-5">31.5 Corrective Action Period</h4>
<p>The period allowed to correct a breach will depend upon the nature of the issue. Simple matters capable of immediate correction may reasonably require prompt action, while matters involving operational, equipment, staffing or other practical changes may require additional time. Apex Feeds will provide a reasonable period having regard to the circumstances rather than applying a single correction period to every type of breach.</p>
<h4 id="s31-6">31.6 Corrective Action</h4>
<p>Corrective action may include, where reasonably relevant:</p>
<ul>
<li>correcting or removing inaccurate advertising;</li>
<li>replacing outdated product information;</li>
<li>correcting use of Apex Feeds branding;</li>
<li>improving product storage or handling;</li>
<li>quarantining affected stock;</li>
<li>correcting an unauthorised online listing;</li>
<li>ceasing supply to an unauthorised third-party reseller;</li>
<li>improving Batch traceability;</li>
<li>providing relevant staff with updated information;</li>
<li>correcting account or business information; or</li>
<li>taking another reasonable step necessary to restore compliance.</li>
</ul>
<p>Corrective action should be proportionate to the identified issue.</p>
<h4 id="s31-7">31.7 Confirmation of Correction</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may reasonably request evidence that a material compliance issue has been corrected. Depending upon the circumstances, this may include a photograph, screenshot, written confirmation or other simple evidence reasonably sufficient to establish correction.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds will not require unnecessary documentation where the issue can otherwise be reasonably confirmed.</p>
<h4 id="s31-8">31.8 Repeated Breaches</h4>
<p>Repeated breaches of the same or similar requirement may be treated more seriously than an isolated occurrence, particularly where Apex Feeds has previously explained the requirement and provided a reasonable opportunity for correction.</p>
<p>In determining whether repeated conduct warrants escalation, Apex Feeds should consider:</p>
<ul>
<li>frequency;</li>
<li>seriousness;</li>
<li>time between incidents;</li>
<li>whether previous corrective action was effective;</li>
<li>whether the conduct appears deliberate or systemic; and</li>
<li>the Retailer’s overall response and cooperation.</li>
</ul>
<p>Multiple unrelated minor administrative errors will not automatically be treated as a serious breach merely because more than one has occurred.</p>
<h4 id="s31-9">31.9 Material Breach</h4>
<p>A material breach is conduct that significantly affects, or creates a substantial risk to, the wholesale relationship, product integrity, animal safety, traceability, Apex Feeds’ legitimate commercial interests or the Retailer’s ability to comply with this Agreement.</p>
<p>Depending upon the circumstances, a material breach may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>deliberate or repeated unauthorised resale or diversion;</li>
<li>deliberate sale through prohibited third-party marketplaces after notice;</li>
<li>serious or repeated product-storage failures;</li>
<li>knowingly selling compromised or recalled product;</li>
<li>deliberate interference with Batch Numbers or Best Before Dates;</li>
<li>material misuse of Apex Feeds branding or intellectual property;</li>
<li>materially false or misleading representations concerning Apex Feeds or its products;</li>
<li>deliberate falsification of a wholesale claim;</li>
<li>material failure to cooperate with a genuine safety, Batch or recall investigation;</li>
<li>serious or repeated biosecurity or legal non-compliance directly affecting Apex Feeds products;</li>
<li>significant misuse of a wholesale account;</li>
<li>repeated failure to remedy an earlier breach after reasonable notice; or</li>
<li>another breach sufficiently serious to materially undermine the wholesale relationship.</li>
</ul>
<p>Whether conduct constitutes a material breach depends upon the circumstances and is not determined solely by the category into which it falls.</p>
<h4 id="s31-10">31.10 Serious Safety or Recall Breach</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may require immediate corrective action where a breach creates a genuine and material risk involving:</p>
<ul>
<li>animal safety;</li>
<li>contamination;</li>
<li>product integrity;</li>
<li>recalled stock;</li>
<li>biosecurity;</li>
<li>unlawful product supply; or</li>
<li>another significant safety or regulatory concern.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apex Feeds is not required to allow potentially unsafe or unlawful conduct to continue merely to provide an ordinary correction period. Immediate termination, where justified, is addressed separately in Section 33.</p>
<h4 id="s31-11">31.11 Temporary Protective Measures</h4>
<p>While a material concern is being investigated or corrected, Apex Feeds may implement reasonable temporary measures necessary to limit the relevant risk.</p>
<p>These may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>temporarily withholding an affected product category;</li>
<li>temporarily disabling affected ordering privileges;</li>
<li>requiring affected stock to be quarantined;</li>
<li>requiring removal of a particular listing or advertisement;</li>
<li>temporarily restricting a particular sales channel; or</li>
<li>another proportionate measure directly connected to the concern.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where reasonably practicable, temporary measures should be limited to the affected product, activity, location or sales channel rather than unnecessarily restricting the entire wholesale account.</p>
<h4 id="s31-12">31.12 Temporary Measures Are Not Automatic Termination</h4>
<p>A temporary restriction, Batch hold, product quarantine, credit suspension or affected-product supply restriction does not, by itself, terminate the Retailer’s Authorised Retailer status. Where the underlying issue is satisfactorily resolved, Apex Feeds should remove the temporary restriction within a reasonable period where there is no continuing basis for it.</p>
<h4 id="s31-13">31.13 Retailer Cooperation</h4>
<p>Retailers must reasonably cooperate with corrective action relating to genuine breaches of this Agreement. Cooperation does not require a Retailer to agree with every allegation made by Apex Feeds or surrender any legal right. A Retailer may provide its own explanation, evidence or proposed corrective action for consideration.</p>
<h4 id="s31-14">31.14 Disputed Breach</h4>
<p>Where the Retailer genuinely disputes an alleged breach, the parties should attempt to resolve the issue by reference to:</p>
<ul>
<li>the terms of this Agreement;</li>
<li>applicable product information;</li>
<li>relevant communications;</li>
<li>available evidence; and</li>
<li>applicable legal or regulatory requirements where relevant.</li>
</ul>
<p>A genuine dispute does not itself constitute an additional breach. Where an immediate safety or legal risk exists, reasonable temporary protective action may continue while the dispute is being resolved.</p>
<h4 id="s31-15">31.15 Voluntary Corrective Action</h4>
<p>Where a Retailer identifies and voluntarily corrects an issue before Apex Feeds raises it, Apex Feeds may take that proactive response into account when determining whether further action is necessary. Retailers are encouraged to raise genuine mistakes or uncertainty early so they can be corrected before becoming larger compliance issues.</p>
<h4 id="s31-16">31.16 Apex Feeds Assistance</h4>
<p>Where appropriate, Apex Feeds may assist a Retailer to correct a compliance issue by providing:</p>
<ul>
<li>updated product information;</li>
<li>current artwork or branding resources;</li>
<li>storage or handling guidance;</li>
<li>live feeder husbandry information;</li>
<li>clarification of authorised sales channels;</li>
<li>updated procedures; or</li>
<li>other reasonable information relevant to correcting the issue.</li>
</ul>
<p>Providing assistance does not transfer the Retailer’s independent compliance responsibilities to Apex Feeds.</p>
<h4 id="s31-17">31.17 Failure to Remedy</h4>
<p>Where a Retailer fails to remedy a material or repeated breach within a reasonable period after receiving clear notice and a reasonable opportunity to do so, Apex Feeds may escalate the matter in accordance with Section 32. This may include suspension of some or all wholesale privileges or, where justified, termination of the wholesale relationship.</p>
<h4 id="s31-18">31.18 Deliberate Circumvention</h4>
<p>A Retailer must not deliberately restructure, disguise or route conduct through another person, account, business, sales channel or location for the purpose of circumventing a requirement of this Agreement. Where Apex Feeds has reasonable evidence of deliberate circumvention, the conduct may be treated according to its substance rather than merely its technical form. This does not prevent legitimate changes to the Retailer’s business structure, ownership, sales channels or operations undertaken in accordance with this Agreement.</p>
<h4 id="s31-19">31.19 Proportionate Enforcement</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds will seek to ensure that enforcement action is reasonably proportionate to the breach. Where a narrower measure is reasonably sufficient to address the issue, Apex Feeds may use that measure rather than unnecessarily suspending or terminating the entire wholesale relationship. Nothing in this clause prevents stronger action where the seriousness, repetition or deliberate nature of the conduct reasonably warrants it.</p>
<h4 id="s31-20">31.20 No Retaliation for Legitimate Complaints</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds will not treat a Retailer’s genuine exercise of a legal right, good-faith product complaint, safety report, regulatory communication or reasonable disagreement with Apex Feeds as a breach merely because the matter is inconvenient or critical of Apex Feeds. This does not protect knowingly false, fraudulent or deliberately misleading conduct.</p>
<h4 id="s31-21">31.21 Records of Material Breaches</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may maintain reasonable internal records of material compliance issues, corrective actions and outcomes for the purpose of administering the wholesale relationship and identifying repeated or systemic concerns. Any personal or confidential information contained in those records must be handled in accordance with applicable requirements and Section 28.</p>
<h4 id="s31-22">31.22 Non-Excludable Rights</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section excludes, restricts or modifies any right, remedy, obligation or liability that cannot lawfully be excluded, restricted or modified.</p>
<h3 id="s32">32. Suspension &amp; Termination</h3>
<p>The wholesale relationship between Apex Feeds and an Authorised Retailer may be suspended or terminated in accordance with this Section where reasonably necessary because of compliance, commercial, operational or other legitimate circumstances.</p>
<p>Where practicable, Apex Feeds will use a measure proportionate to the issue and will distinguish between temporary restrictions, suspension of particular wholesale privileges and termination of the entire Authorised Retailer relationship. Nothing in this Section requires Apex Feeds to continue supplying a Retailer where there are reasonable grounds to suspend or terminate the wholesale relationship.</p>
<h4 id="s32-1">32.1 Temporary Suspension</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may temporarily suspend some or all wholesale privileges where there are reasonable grounds to do so.</p>
<p>Depending upon the circumstances, grounds may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>an unresolved material breach;</li>
<li>repeated non-compliance after reasonable corrective action;</li>
<li>overdue undisputed amounts;</li>
<li>misuse or suspected unauthorised use of the wholesale account;</li>
<li>suspected unauthorised resupply or product diversion;</li>
<li>a material product-storage or handling concern;</li>
<li>a safety, contamination or product-integrity concern;</li>
<li>a material biosecurity or regulatory concern;</li>
<li>failure to cooperate with a genuine Batch, withdrawal or recall matter;</li>
<li>reasonable concern regarding the Retailer’s continuing eligibility for Authorised Retailer status;</li>
<li>an unresolved change of ownership, legal entity or ABN;</li>
<li>suspected fraudulent or materially misleading activity; or</li>
<li>another circumstance where temporary suspension is reasonably necessary to protect legitimate interests under this Agreement.</li>
</ul>
<p>Suspension should not be used arbitrarily or for a purpose inconsistent with applicable law.</p>
<h4 id="s32-2">32.2 Scope of Suspension</h4>
<p>A suspension may apply to:</p>
<ul>
<li>wholesale ordering generally;</li>
<li>a particular product or product category;</li>
<li>live feeder supply;</li>
<li>frozen-product supply;</li>
<li>credit facilities;</li>
<li>a particular sales channel;</li>
<li>a particular Retailer location; or</li>
<li>another affected aspect of the wholesale relationship.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where a narrower suspension is reasonably sufficient to address the concern, Apex Feeds should avoid unnecessarily suspending unrelated compliant activities.</p>
<h4 id="s32-3">32.3 Credit Suspension</h4>
<p>Suspension or withdrawal of a Retailer’s credit facility does not necessarily suspend or terminate the wholesale account. Where credit is suspended or withdrawn, Apex Feeds may require future orders to be prepaid in accordance with Section 4. Amounts already owing remain payable according to the applicable invoice and credit terms.</p>
<h4 id="s32-4">32.4 Notice of Suspension</h4>
<p>Where reasonably practicable, Apex Feeds will notify the Retailer of:</p>
<ul>
<li>the fact that a suspension has been imposed;</li>
<li>the general reason for the suspension;</li>
<li>the scope of the suspension;</li>
<li>any corrective action reasonably required; and</li>
<li>any information reasonably required before the suspension can be reviewed or lifted.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where an immediate safety, fraud, legal, biosecurity, account-security or product-integrity concern exists, Apex Feeds may impose a temporary suspension before completing its investigation and provide further information as soon as reasonably practicable.</p>
<h4 id="s32-5">32.5 Review of Suspension</h4>
<p>A temporary suspension should remain in place only for so long as there is a reasonable basis for it. Where the Retailer provides information, remedies the issue or otherwise resolves the concern, Apex Feeds will review the suspension within a reasonable period.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds may:</p>
<ul>
<li>lift the suspension;</li>
<li>reduce its scope;</li>
<li>continue it while a genuine issue remains unresolved;</li>
<li>impose reasonable corrective conditions; or</li>
<li>where justified, proceed toward termination.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s32-6">32.6 Suspension Does Not Cancel Existing Obligations</h4>
<p>Suspension of wholesale privileges does not automatically:</p>
<ul>
<li>cancel amounts already owing;</li>
<li>cancel an accepted order already dispatched;</li>
<li>remove confidentiality obligations;</li>
<li>remove product-storage or recall obligations;</li>
<li>prevent legitimate claims relating to previously supplied stock; or</li>
<li>terminate provisions intended to continue during or after suspension.</li>
</ul>
<p>The treatment of accepted but undispatched orders will depend upon the reason for the suspension and the circumstances of the order.</p>
<h4 id="s32-7">32.7 Accepted Orders During Suspension</h4>
<p>Where an order has been accepted but not yet dispatched when a suspension occurs, Apex Feeds may place the order on hold where the reason for suspension reasonably affects fulfilment. Where the issue is resolved, the order may proceed. Where the order cannot reasonably proceed, Apex Feeds will provide an appropriate refund, credit or other adjustment for amounts paid in respect of products that will not be supplied.</p>
<h4 id="s32-8">32.8 Termination Following Breach</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may terminate the Authorised Retailer relationship where:</p>
<ul>
<li>the Retailer commits a material breach that is capable of remedy and fails to remedy it within a reasonable period after clear notice;</li>
<li>repeated breaches demonstrate that previous corrective action has not been effective;</li>
<li>the Retailer repeatedly refuses to comply with material requirements of the Agreement;</li>
<li>a suspension cannot reasonably resolve the underlying issue; or</li>
<li>the circumstances otherwise materially undermine the basis upon which Authorised Retailer status was granted.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where the breach is capable of reasonable correction and does not require immediate termination, the corrective process under Section 31 should ordinarily be followed first.</p>
<h4 id="s32-9">32.9 Termination for Loss of Eligibility</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may terminate Authorised Retailer status where the Retailer no longer materially satisfies the eligibility requirements of the wholesale program. T</p>
<p>his may include circumstances where the Retailer:</p>
<ul>
<li>permanently ceases operating its approved physical retail business;</li>
<li>becomes an online-only or otherwise ineligible business under the wholesale program;</li>
<li>transfers the business to a different legal entity that has not been approved;</li>
<li>no longer holds a licence or authorisation materially necessary to lawfully sell the relevant products; or</li>
<li>undergoes another material change that means the basis for its original wholesale approval no longer exists.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where the issue affects only a particular product category, location or activity, Apex Feeds may restrict that aspect rather than terminate the entire account where reasonably appropriate.</p>
<h4 id="s32-10">32.10 Ordinary Termination by Apex Feeds</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may terminate the wholesale relationship for a legitimate commercial or operational reason that does not arise from Retailer misconduct by providing the Retailer with at least 30 days’ written notice. Such reasons may include a genuine restructuring of Apex Feeds’ wholesale program, discontinuation of a distribution model, withdrawal from a market or region, substantial operational change or another legitimate business reason affecting the continuation of the wholesale relationship.</p>
<p>This clause must not be used for an unlawful purpose or to penalise a Retailer merely for exercising a legal right, making a genuine complaint or independently determining its retail prices.</p>
<h4 id="s32-11">32.11 Ordinary Termination by the Retailer</h4>
<p>The Retailer may terminate its participation in the Apex Feeds wholesale program by providing Apex Feeds with 30 days’ written notice. Apex Feeds may agree to an earlier termination date where requested. Termination does not remove obligations relating to amounts already owing, existing claims, remaining stock or other provisions intended to continue after termination.</p>
<h4 id="s32-12">32.12 Closure of Retail Business</h4>
<p>Where a Retailer permanently closes its business, it should notify Apex Feeds as soon as reasonably practicable. Apex Feeds may agree to terminate the wholesale relationship without requiring the full ordinary notice period where continued operation of the account serves no practical purpose. Remaining genuine Apex Feeds stock may be dealt with in accordance with Section 34 and the continuing-stock provisions of this Agreement.</p>
<h4 id="s32-13">32.13 Insolvency and Financial Difficulty</h4>
<p>Where the Retailer experiences material financial difficulty, Apex Feeds may reasonably protect its exposure by suspending credit, requiring prepayment or restricting further supply. Financial difficulty alone does not require Apex Feeds to terminate the wholesale relationship where a workable trading arrangement remains reasonably available.</p>
<p>Any action relating to insolvency, administration, restructuring, liquidation or similar circumstances remains subject to applicable law.</p>
<h4 id="s32-14">32.14 Outstanding Amounts</h4>
<p>Termination does not extinguish amounts properly owing for products or services supplied before termination. Amounts already due remain payable according to their applicable terms. Amounts not yet due remain payable when they become due, subject to any valid credit, claim, adjustment or other right available under this Agreement or applicable law.</p>
<h4 id="s32-15">32.15 Existing Claims</h4>
<p>Termination or suspension does not prevent either party from pursuing a genuine claim relating to conduct, products, orders or circumstances arising before termination. A Retailer remains entitled to submit a legitimate manufacturing, quality or other applicable claim concerning stock previously purchased from Apex Feeds, subject to the applicable requirements of this Agreement and law.</p>
<h4 id="s32-16">32.16 Existing Stock</h4>
<p>Suspension or termination of Authorised Retailer status does not, by itself, make genuine Apex Feeds stock already purchased by the Retailer unlawful or unsuitable for sale. Unless the stock is subject to recall, withdrawal, legal restriction or another genuine safety or regulatory issue, remaining stock may be dealt with in accordance with Section 34.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds is not automatically required to repurchase remaining saleable inventory merely because the wholesale relationship ends.</p>
<h4 id="s32-17">32.17 No Automatic Repurchase Obligation</h4>
<p>Except where required by law, an applicable product recall or an express written agreement, Apex Feeds is not required to repurchase ordinary saleable inventory held by a Retailer when the wholesale relationship is suspended or terminated. Nothing prevents Apex Feeds and the Retailer from voluntarily agreeing to a stock return or repurchase where commercially appropriate.</p>
<h4 id="s32-18">32.18 Termination Is Not Retrospective</h4>
<p>Termination ends the Retailer’s ongoing Authorised Retailer relationship from the applicable termination date. It does not retrospectively invalidate legitimate purchases, advertising, sales or other conduct that occurred while the Retailer was properly authorised and compliant with the requirements then applying.</p>
<h4 id="s32-19">32.19 Communication of Termination</h4>
<p>Notice of termination should be provided in writing through the Retailer’s nominated wholesale contact details or another established written business communication channel.</p>
<p>The notice should identify:</p>
<ul>
<li>the effective termination date;</li>
<li>whether termination is ordinary termination or arises from breach or eligibility;</li>
<li>any immediate restrictions applying before that date;</li>
<li>treatment of outstanding accepted orders where relevant; and</li>
<li>any reasonable steps required concerning Authorised Retailer representations or remaining stock.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s32-20">32.20 No Retaliatory Termination</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds will not suspend or terminate a Retailer merely because the Retailer:</p>
<ul>
<li>sells or advertises Apex Feeds products below the recommended retail price;</li>
<li>makes a genuine warranty, quality or product complaint;</li>
<li>reports a genuine safety concern;</li>
<li>communicates lawfully with a regulator;</li>
<li>exercises a legal right;</li>
<li>raises a genuine dispute with Apex Feeds; or</li>
<li>declines to participate in an optional Apex Feeds promotion.</li>
</ul>
<p>This does not prevent suspension or termination where separate legitimate grounds exist.</p>
<h4 id="s32-21">32.21 Immediate Termination</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section prevents immediate termination where circumstances are sufficiently serious that continued Authorised Retailer status cannot reasonably be maintained. The circumstances and procedure for immediate termination are set out in Section 33.</p>
<h4 id="s32-22">32.22 Proportionate Exercise of Rights</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds will exercise suspension and termination rights reasonably and having regard to the circumstances giving rise to the action. Where a less restrictive measure would reasonably address the concern, Apex Feeds may use that measure instead of terminating the entire wholesale relationship.</p>
<p>This does not require Apex Feeds to repeatedly provide corrective opportunities where previous attempts have failed or where the conduct is sufficiently serious to justify stronger action.</p>
<h4 id="s32-23">32.23 Non-Excludable Rights</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section excludes, restricts or modifies any right, remedy, obligation or liability that cannot lawfully be excluded, restricted or modified.</p>
<h3 id="s33">33. Immediate Termination</h3>
<p>Apex Feeds may terminate a Retailer’s Authorised Retailer status with immediate effect where the Retailer engages in conduct sufficiently serious that Apex Feeds cannot reasonably be expected to continue the wholesale relationship or provide an ordinary corrective period under Sections 31 and 32. Immediate termination is reserved for serious circumstances and will not ordinarily be used for minor, accidental or reasonably remediable breaches.</p>
<p>Where reasonably practicable, Apex Feeds will consider available information and the Retailer’s explanation before exercising an immediate termination right. This does not require Apex Feeds to delay urgent protective action where a genuine safety, legal, fraud, biosecurity or product-integrity risk exists.</p>
<h4 id="s33-1">33.1 Serious Fraud or Dishonesty</h4>
<p>Immediate termination may be appropriate where the Retailer engages in deliberate fraud or material dishonesty directly connected with Apex Feeds or the wholesale relationship.</p>
<p>This may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>knowingly falsifying wholesale claims;</li>
<li>fabricating photographs, records or evidence in support of a claim;</li>
<li>deliberately providing materially false information to obtain wholesale approval, credit or another commercial benefit;</li>
<li>deliberate misuse of another business’s wholesale account or identity; *</li>
</ul>
<p>intentional falsification of Batch Numbers, Best Before Dates or product identification; or</p>
<ul>
<li>other serious fraudulent conduct materially affecting Apex Feeds.</li>
</ul>
<p>A genuine mistake, disputed claim or reasonable difference of interpretation does not constitute fraud merely because Apex Feeds disagrees with the Retailer.</p>
<h4 id="s33-2">33.2 Knowingly Selling Recalled or Unsafe Stock</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may immediately terminate where a Retailer knowingly continues to sell, supply or distribute product after receiving clear notice that the affected stock:</p>
<ul>
<li>is subject to a product recall;</li>
<li>must not be sold because of a confirmed material safety concern;</li>
<li>is prohibited from sale by law or lawful regulatory direction; or</li>
<li>otherwise presents a known serious product or animal-safety risk.</li>
</ul>
<p>An inadvertent sale caused by a genuine administrative mistake will not automatically justify immediate termination where the Retailer acts promptly to correct the issue and cooperate with the applicable recall or safety process.</p>
<h4 id="s33-3">33.3 Deliberate Product Diversion or Unauthorised Resupply</h4>
<p>Immediate termination may be appropriate where there is reasonable evidence that the Retailer has deliberately established, concealed or continued a material arrangement to divert Apex Feeds wholesale stock to unauthorised resellers or businesses contrary to Sections 8 and 9. In determining whether immediate termination is appropriate, Apex Feeds may consider the scale, frequency, deliberate nature and commercial purpose of the conduct. An isolated mistaken supply or genuine misunderstanding should ordinarily be dealt with through the corrective procedures in Section 31 where reasonably appropriate.</p>
<h4 id="s33-4">33.4 Deliberate Circumvention</h4>
<p>Immediate termination may be appropriate where the Retailer deliberately uses another person, business, legal entity, account, location or sales channel to conceal conduct that would constitute a serious breach if undertaken directly. Apex Feeds may consider the substance and purpose of the arrangement rather than merely its formal structure. This does not restrict legitimate business restructuring, transfers or operational arrangements conducted transparently and in accordance with this Agreement.</p>
<h4 id="s33-5">33.5 Serious Product Tampering or Misrepresentation</h4>
<p>Immediate termination may be appropriate where the Retailer deliberately:</p>
<ul>
<li>materially alters an Apex Feeds manufactured product and continues representing it as the original Apex Feeds product;</li>
<li>applies Apex Feeds branding or packaging to a non-Apex product;</li>
<li>falsifies or replaces product identification; * intentionally conceals important safety, storage, ingredient or Batch information;</li>
<li>sells counterfeit or substituted product as Apex Feeds stock; or</li>
<li>otherwise materially misrepresents the identity or origin of a product in a manner capable of causing serious customer, animal, legal or reputational harm.</li>
</ul>
<p>This clause does not restrict permitted repacking and commercial handling of live feeder insects under Section 17.</p>
<h4 id="s33-6">33.6 Serious Brand or Intellectual Property Misuse</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may immediately terminate where the Retailer deliberately engages in serious misuse of Apex Feeds branding or intellectual property that materially misrepresents the Retailer’s identity, falsely represents itself as Apex Feeds, creates a counterfeit Apex Feeds product or causes another substantial commercial or reputational risk.</p>
<p>Ordinary artwork errors, incorrect logo use or other reasonably remediable branding issues should ordinarily be addressed under Section 31 rather than immediate termination.</p>
<h4 id="s33-7">33.7 Serious Safety or Biosecurity Conduct</h4>
<p>Immediate termination may be appropriate where a Retailer knowingly or recklessly engages in conduct involving Apex Feeds products that creates a serious and material animal-safety, biosecurity, contamination or regulatory risk.</p>
<p>This may include knowingly supplying a prohibited live feeder species into a jurisdiction where the Retailer is aware that supply is unlawful, or deliberately ignoring a lawful stop-sale or quarantine direction. Where the issue arises from genuine uncertainty concerning a legal or biosecurity requirement, Apex Feeds should ordinarily seek clarification and use proportionate protective measures rather than treating uncertainty itself as deliberate misconduct.</p>
<h4 id="s33-8">33.8 Serious Unlawful Conduct Connected With Apex Feeds</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may immediately terminate where the Retailer engages in serious unlawful conduct directly connected with the purchase, storage, advertising, sale, supply or representation of Apex Feeds products and that conduct materially affects the lawful continuation of the wholesale relationship. This clause does not give Apex Feeds a general right to terminate because of allegations or unrelated matters concerning the Retailer’s business or its owners.</p>
<h4 id="s33-9">33.9 Serious Account or Security Misuse</h4>
<p>Immediate termination may be appropriate where the Retailer deliberately:</p>
<ul>
<li>provides wholesale account access to an unauthorised third party for improper commercial use;</li>
<li>uses another Retailer’s account without authority;</li>
<li>fraudulently obtains products or credit;</li>
<li>deliberately manipulates account information to circumvent wholesale requirements; or</li>
<li>engages in another serious misuse of Apex Feeds’ wholesale systems.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ordinary account-security incidents should be addressed according to their actual circumstances rather than automatically treated as deliberate misconduct.</p>
<h4 id="s33-10">33.10 Deliberate Refusal to Cooperate With Critical Safety Action</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds may immediately terminate where a Retailer deliberately refuses to comply with a clear, lawful and reasonable direction necessary to manage an urgent:</p>
<ul>
<li>product recall;</li>
<li>serious contamination concern;</li>
<li>material animal-safety risk;</li>
<li>Batch traceability emergency;</li>
<li>biosecurity issue; or</li>
<li>regulatory requirement, where continued refusal creates a material risk.</li>
</ul>
<p>A genuine request for clarification or reasonable disagreement concerning how an instruction should be implemented does not constitute deliberate refusal where the Retailer continues taking reasonable steps to protect affected stock, customers and animals.</p>
<h4 id="s33-11">33.11 Repeated Serious Breach Following Previous Action</h4>
<p>Immediate termination may also be appropriate where a Retailer repeats serious conduct after Apex Feeds has previously:</p>
<ul>
<li>clearly identified the same or substantially similar breach;</li>
<li>explained the applicable requirement;</li>
<li>provided a reasonable opportunity for correction; and</li>
<li>warned that further serious non-compliance may result in suspension or termination.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apex Feeds is not required to repeatedly restart the corrective-action process where previous reasonable attempts have failed.</p>
<h4 id="s33-12">33.12 Immediate Protective Suspension Before Decision</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds receives credible information suggesting that grounds for immediate termination may exist, it may first immediately suspend some or all wholesale privileges while the matter is investigated. This allows Apex Feeds to protect customers, animals, products, accounts or the supply chain without necessarily making a final termination decision before relevant information has been considered. Where the allegation is not substantiated, Apex Feeds should remove the suspension within a reasonable period where no other grounds for restriction remain.</p>
<h4 id="s33-13">33.13 Reasonable Opportunity to Explain</h4>
<p>Where circumstances permit without creating an unreasonable safety, legal or commercial risk, Apex Feeds should provide the Retailer with a reasonable opportunity to respond to a serious allegation before finalising immediate termination.</p>
<p>This requirement does not prevent Apex Feeds from first suspending supply, quarantining affected stock, disabling account access or taking another reasonable protective action.</p>
<h4 id="s33-14">33.14 Immediate Termination Notice</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds terminates Authorised Retailer status immediately, it will provide written notice identifying:</p>
<ul>
<li>that Authorised Retailer status has been terminated;</li>
<li>the effective date of termination;</li>
<li>the general basis for the decision;</li>
<li>treatment of outstanding accepted orders where applicable;</li>
<li>any immediate product, recall or safety requirements that continue to apply; and</li>
<li>the Retailer’s obligations concerning remaining stock and Authorised Retailer representations.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apex Feeds is not required to disclose legally privileged, confidential third-party or regulator-restricted information where disclosure would be unlawful or inappropriate.</p>
<h4 id="s33-15">33.15 Existing Financial Obligations</h4>
<p>Immediate termination does not extinguish amounts properly owing for products or services supplied before termination. Similarly, termination does not prevent the Retailer from pursuing a genuine credit, refund, manufacturing-defect claim or other right concerning previously supplied products.</p>
<h4 id="s33-16">33.16 Remaining Genuine Stock</h4>
<p>Immediate termination does not automatically make genuine Apex Feeds stock already lawfully purchased by the Retailer unsuitable for sale. Unless the stock is recalled, withdrawn, prohibited, compromised or otherwise subject to a genuine safety or legal restriction, its treatment following termination is governed by Section 34. This applies even where termination arose from Retailer misconduct, unless continued sale of the particular stock would itself create a legitimate safety, legal or product-integrity concern.</p>
<h4 id="s33-17">33.17 Continuing Safety and Recall Obligations</h4>
<p>Termination does not release either party from obligations reasonably necessary to manage products already supplied. The former Retailer must continue to cooperate with applicable product recalls, withdrawals, safety investigations and Batch traceability requirements relating to stock supplied while it was an Authorised Retailer.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds must similarly continue to meet its applicable responsibilities concerning products it previously supplied.</p>
<h4 id="s33-18">33.18 No Immediate Termination for Lawful Retail Pricing</h4>
<p>For clarity, selling or advertising Apex Feeds products below Apex Feeds’ Recommended Retail Price does not, by itself, constitute grounds for immediate termination. Nothing in this Section may be used to impose or enforce a minimum resale or advertised price contrary to applicable law.</p>
<h4 id="s33-19">33.19 No Immediate Termination for Protected or Legitimate Conduct</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds will not immediately terminate a Retailer merely because the Retailer:</p>
<ul>
<li>makes a genuine product or quality complaint;</li>
<li>reports a genuine safety concern;</li>
<li>raises a reasonable contractual dispute;</li>
<li>communicates lawfully with a regulator;</li>
<li>exercises a legal right;</li>
<li>provides genuine critical feedback to Apex Feeds; or</li>
<li>declines to participate in an optional promotion.</li>
</ul>
<p>This does not prevent termination where separate serious grounds genuinely exist.</p>
<h4 id="s33-20">33.20 Proportionality</h4>
<p>Immediate termination should be reserved for circumstances where the seriousness, deliberate nature, repetition or consequences of the conduct reasonably justify ending the wholesale relationship without the ordinary notice or corrective period. Where suspension or corrective action would reasonably and adequately address the concern, Apex Feeds may use those measures instead.</p>
<h4 id="s33-21">33.21 Non-Excludable Rights</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section excludes, restricts or modifies any right, remedy, obligation or liability that cannot lawfully be excluded, restricted or modified.</p>
<h3 id="s34">34. Effect of Termination</h3>
<p>Upon termination of an Authorised Retailer’s wholesale relationship with Apex Feeds, the Retailer’s authority to obtain new wholesale supply and represent itself as a current Authorised Apex Feeds Retailer ends from the effective termination date.</p>
<p>Termination does not retrospectively invalidate legitimate purchases or sales made while the Retailer was authorised and does not, by itself, prevent the Retailer from selling genuine remaining Apex Feeds stock that it lawfully purchased before termination. The following provisions apply unless a product is subject to a recall, withdrawal, safety restriction, legal prohibition or other specific requirement that prevents continued sale.</p>
<h4 id="s34-1">34.1 Wholesale Account Access</h4>
<p>From the effective termination date, Apex Feeds may close or disable the Retailer’s wholesale account and associated ordering privileges.</p>
<p>The former Retailer will no longer be entitled to:</p>
<ul>
<li>place new wholesale orders;</li>
<li>access account-specific wholesale pricing;</li>
<li>use wholesale credit facilities;</li>
<li>obtain benefits available only to current Authorised Retailers; or</li>
<li>represent that it continues to hold current Authorised Retailer status.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apex Feeds may retain account and transaction records as reasonably necessary for legal, accounting, warranty, claim, recall and legitimate business purposes.</p>
<h4 id="s34-2">34.2 Authorised Retailer Status</h4>
<p>From the effective termination date, the former Retailer must cease representing itself as: “Authorised Apex Feeds Retailer” or using another statement that reasonably implies that a current authorised wholesale relationship continues.</p>
<p>This does not prevent the former Retailer from accurately stating that it sells or stocks genuine Apex Feeds products while genuine remaining inventory is being sold.</p>
<h4 id="s34-3">34.3 Removal of Authorised Retailer References</h4>
<p>The former Retailer must remove current Authorised Retailer representations from active:</p>
<ul>
<li>website pages;</li>
<li>social media profiles or biographies;</li>
<li>store signage;</li>
<li>directories;</li>
<li>advertising;</li>
<li>email signatures; and</li>
<li>other current promotional material, within a reasonable period following termination.</li>
</ul>
<p>Historical social media posts, photographs or archived material created while the Retailer was genuinely authorised do not need to be deleted merely because the wholesale relationship has subsequently ended, provided they are not being actively used in a misleading manner to represent continuing authorisation.</p>
<h4 id="s34-4">34.4 Apex Feeds Retailer Listings</h4>
<p>Following termination, Apex Feeds may remove the former Retailer from its current Authorised Retailer listings, store locators, retailer directories or other materials identifying businesses presently participating in the wholesale program.</p>
<p>Historical Apex Feeds content referring to the Retailer while it was genuinely authorised does not necessarily need to be removed unless continued publication would be misleading in the circumstances.</p>
<h4 id="s34-5">34.5 Remaining Genuine Stock</h4>
<p>Unless otherwise restricted under this Agreement or applicable law, the former Retailer may continue selling genuine Apex Feeds products purchased before termination while those products:</p>
<ul>
<li>remain within their applicable Best Before Date;</li>
<li>remain suitable for sale;</li>
<li>have been stored and handled appropriately;</li>
<li>retain required product identification;</li>
<li>are not subject to recall or withdrawal; and</li>
<li>can otherwise lawfully be sold.</li>
</ul>
<p>Termination alone does not require otherwise saleable genuine stock to be destroyed, returned or removed from sale.</p>
<h4 id="s34-6">34.6 Clearance of Remaining Stock</h4>
<p>A former Retailer may discount or clear genuine remaining Apex Feeds stock at prices determined independently by the Retailer.</p>
<p>This includes reasonable:</p>
<ul>
<li>closing-down sales;</li>
<li>liquidation or clearance sales;</li>
<li>short-dated discounts;</li>
<li>end-of-line discounts;</li>
<li>storewide sales; or</li>
<li>other genuine stock-clearance activity.</li>
</ul>
<p>Apex Feeds does not impose a minimum clearance or resale price. Any advertising must accurately represent the promotion as the former Retailer’s own offer and must not falsely represent it as an Apex Feeds promotion.</p>
<h4 id="s34-7">34.7 No Mandatory Destruction of Paid Stock</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds will not require a former Retailer to destroy genuine, lawful and saleable Apex Feeds stock merely because the wholesale relationship has ended.</p>
<p>Destruction, quarantine, return or cessation of sale may still be required where justified by:</p>
<ul>
<li>product recall;</li>
<li>product withdrawal;</li>
<li>contamination;</li>
<li>safety concerns;</li>
<li>expired stock;</li>
<li>legal or regulatory requirements;</li>
<li>compromised product integrity; or</li>
<li>another legitimate reason concerning the particular stock.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s34-8">34.8 No Automatic Stock Repurchase</h4>
<p>Apex Feeds is not required to repurchase remaining stock merely because the wholesale relationship has ended. Apex Feeds and the former Retailer may voluntarily agree to the return, transfer, credit or repurchase of particular stock where both parties consider that commercially appropriate. Nothing in this clause affects an otherwise valid product claim, recall remedy or non-excludable legal right.</p>
<h4 id="s34-9">34.9 Continued Use of Apex Feeds Product Information</h4>
<p>While genuine remaining Apex Feeds stock is being sold, the former Retailer may continue using Apex Feeds product information reasonably necessary to identify, describe and sell that stock.</p>
<p>This may include applicable:</p>
<ul>
<li>product names;</li>
<li>product photographs;</li>
<li>product descriptions;</li>
<li>long-form descriptions;</li>
<li>ingredient and nutritional information;</li>
<li>preparation and feeding instructions; and</li>
<li>other Apex Feeds materials previously supplied for ordinary retailer advertising.</li>
</ul>
<p>Such use must remain accurate and must not imply that the former Retailer continues to hold Authorised Retailer status.</p>
<h4 id="s34-10">34.10 Continued Use of Apex Feeds Branding</h4>
<p>The former Retailer may use Apex Feeds branding to the limited extent reasonably necessary to identify and advertise genuine remaining Apex Feeds stock.</p>
<p>This does not authorise the former Retailer to:</p>
<ul>
<li>present itself as Apex Feeds;</li>
<li>claim continuing Authorised Retailer status;</li>
<li>create new Apex-branded products;</li>
<li>apply Apex branding to non-Apex products;</li>
<li>materially modify Apex logos or artwork; or</li>
<li>otherwise create a false impression of an ongoing wholesale relationship.</li>
</ul>
<p>Once genuine remaining Apex Feeds inventory has been sold or otherwise disposed of, continued commercial use of Apex Feeds branding must cease unless separately authorised.</p>
<h4 id="s34-11">34.11 Existing Advertising Materials</h4>
<p>A former Retailer is not required to immediately destroy posters, photographs, product cards or other Apex Feeds promotional material solely because the wholesale relationship ends. Materials reasonably required to sell genuine remaining stock may continue to be used during the sell-through period, provided any statement of current Authorised Retailer status is removed or covered where reasonably practicable. Once remaining stock has been sold, Apex-specific promotional materials should be removed from active commercial display unless otherwise authorised.</p>
<h4 id="s34-12">34.12 No Fixed Sell-Through Period</h4>
<p>Unless a specific safety, legal, regulatory or product-integrity reason requires otherwise, Apex Feeds does not impose an arbitrary fixed period within which genuine remaining stock must be sold following termination. The former Retailer may reasonably sell through that stock while it remains within its applicable Best Before Date and otherwise suitable and lawful for sale. This clause does not create a right to obtain replacement stock or further wholesale supply after termination.</p>
<h4 id="s34-13">34.13 Online Sale of Remaining Stock</h4>
<p>A former Retailer may continue selling genuine remaining stock through the Retailer’s own existing business website and ordinary store social media channels during the sell-through period, provided the advertising does not imply continuing Authorised Retailer status. Termination does not create permission to move remaining stock onto a third-party marketplace or unauthorised resale channel that was prohibited while the Retailer was authorised.</p>
<h4 id="s34-14">34.14 Remaining Live Feeders</h4>
<p>Termination does not require the former Retailer to destroy or unnecessarily dispose of healthy live feeder insects lawfully purchased before termination.</p>
<p>The former Retailer may continue to:</p>
<ul>
<li>care for them;</li>
<li>use them internally;</li>
<li>breed from them where lawful;</li>
<li>sell remaining live feeder stock to end customers where lawful; and</li>
<li>divide bulk live feeders into smaller retail quantities as otherwise permitted under Section 17.</li>
</ul>
<p>All applicable animal welfare, biosecurity and legal requirements continue to apply.</p>
<h4 id="s34-15">34.15 Outstanding Orders</h4>
<p>Orders accepted before termination will be dealt with according to Sections 32 and 33 and the circumstances giving rise to termination.</p>
<p>Apex Feeds may:</p>
<ul>
<li>complete an unaffected accepted order;</li>
<li>place an order temporarily on hold;</li>
<li>cancel an order where fulfilment can no longer reasonably proceed; or</li>
<li>agree another arrangement with the Retailer.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where Apex Feeds cancels prepaid products that will not be supplied, the applicable amount paid for those products will be refunded or credited as appropriate.</p>
<h4 id="s34-16">34.16 Outstanding Accounts</h4>
<p>Termination does not extinguish valid amounts owing between the parties. The former Retailer remains responsible for properly payable invoices arising before termination, subject to valid credits, claims, adjustments and applicable legal rights. Apex Feeds remains responsible for valid refunds, credits or other amounts properly owing to the Retailer.</p>
<h4 id="s34-17">34.17 Existing Product Claims</h4>
<p>Termination does not prevent a former Retailer from making a genuine claim concerning products supplied while it was an Authorised Retailer. A claim will be assessed according to the applicable provisions of this Agreement and the circumstances of the original supply. Apex Feeds must not reject an otherwise valid manufacturing, freight or quality claim merely because the wholesale relationship has subsequently ended.</p>
<h4 id="s34-18">34.18 Customer Returns Following Termination</h4>
<p>Where a customer returns Apex Feeds stock originally purchased from the former Retailer, the former Retailer remains responsible for its obligations as the original seller to the extent required by applicable law. Where the underlying issue represents a genuine Apex Feeds manufacturing or supply claim, the former Retailer may continue to seek an applicable remedy from Apex Feeds under this Agreement.</p>
<h4 id="s34-19">34.19 Product Withdrawals and Recalls</h4>
<p>Termination does not end obligations concerning Apex Feeds products already supplied. A former Retailer must continue to reasonably cooperate with any applicable Batch hold, product withdrawal or recall concerning stock supplied during the wholesale relationship. Apex Feeds must continue providing the former Retailer with information reasonably necessary to manage affected stock and any applicable customer-level recall.</p>
<h4 id="s34-20">34.20 Batch Traceability</h4>
<p>Reasonable Batch traceability obligations relating to products supplied before termination continue for so long as they are reasonably necessary for legitimate quality, safety, claim, withdrawal or recall purposes. Termination does not require the former Retailer to create records that it was not otherwise required to maintain.</p>
<h4 id="s34-21">34.21 Confidential Information</h4>
<p>Termination does not remove confidentiality obligations that survive under Section 28.</p>
<p>However, confidentiality must not be used to prevent the former Retailer from:</p>
<ul>
<li>accurately selling genuine remaining stock;</li>
<li>complying with legal obligations;</li>
<li>communicating necessary safety or recall information;</li>
<li>obtaining professional advice; or</li>
<li>exercising a legal right.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s34-22">34.22 Return or Destruction of Confidential Materials</h4>
<p>Where reasonably requested, genuinely confidential Apex Feeds materials no longer required for the sale of remaining stock or another legitimate purpose must be returned, deleted or destroyed in accordance with Section 28. The former Retailer may retain ordinary invoices, accounting records, transaction records and other material reasonably or legally required for its records.</p>
<h4 id="s34-23">34.23 Retailer-Created Content</h4>
<p>Photographs, videos, graphics or other original content created and owned by the Retailer remain the Retailer’s property following termination. Termination does not transfer ownership of that material to Apex Feeds. Any continuing Apex Feeds use of retailer-owned content remains subject to Section 10 and any permission previously granted.</p>
<h4 id="s34-24">34.24 Apex Feeds Sharing of Historical Retailer Content</h4>
<p>Where Apex Feeds previously shared or reposted public Retailer content through normal social media functionality while the Retailer was authorised, termination does not necessarily require deletion of historical posts. However, Apex Feeds should not continue actively representing the former Retailer as currently authorised after termination.</p>
<h4 id="s34-25">34.25 No Continuing Authority to Act for Apex Feeds</h4>
<p>Following termination, the former Retailer has no authority to:</p>
<ul>
<li>enter agreements on behalf of Apex Feeds;</li>
<li>make commitments on behalf of Apex Feeds;</li>
<li>provide warranties as though issued directly by Apex Feeds;</li>
<li>represent itself as an Apex Feeds employee, agent or representative; or</li>
<li>otherwise bind Apex Feeds.</li>
</ul>
<p>Nothing in this clause prevents the former Retailer from providing ordinary customer service concerning products it previously sold.</p>
<h4 id="s34-26">34.26 Continuing Provisions</h4>
<p>Termination does not affect provisions which, by their nature or express wording, are intended to continue after the wholesale relationship ends.</p>
<p>This includes, where applicable, provisions concerning:</p>
<ul>
<li>outstanding payments;</li>
<li>existing claims;</li>
<li>confidentiality;</li>
<li>intellectual property;</li>
<li>remaining stock;</li>
<li>product safety;</li>
<li>Batch traceability;</li>
<li>withdrawals and recalls;</li>
<li>customer obligations relating to previous sales;</li>
<li>dispute or enforcement rights; and</li>
<li>other accrued rights and obligations.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="s34-27">34.27 Accrued Rights</h4>
<p>Termination does not extinguish a right, obligation, liability or remedy that arose before the effective termination date. Neither party is released from responsibility for conduct occurring before termination merely because the commercial relationship has subsequently ended.</p>
<h4 id="s34-28">34.28 Cooperation During Exit</h4>
<p>Where reasonably necessary, Apex Feeds and the former Retailer should cooperate to conclude outstanding matters associated with the wholesale relationship, including accepted orders, credits, claims, recalls and remaining-stock questions.</p>
<p>Neither party is required to continue an ongoing commercial relationship merely because administrative matters remain to be finalised.</p>
<h4 id="s34-29">34.29 Non-Excludable Rights</h4>
<p>Nothing in this Section excludes, restricts or modifies any right, remedy, obligation or liability that cannot lawfully be excluded, restricted or modified.</p>

